today's speaker, philosophy.

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:10.910
Today's speaker, I'm absolutely
delighted to introduce him,

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:15.150
Professor Alex Paseau,

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:19.560
who is a professor of
philosophy at Oxford,

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.260
and it is a particular pleasure for me

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:25.346
because he is also a very good friend,

00:02:25.346 --> 00:02:29.070
and so inference is

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:31.470
a logical philosophical term

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:34.740
that Professor Paseau will show us

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.750
has been used to describe the process

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:39.570
of deriving conclusions from premises,

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:41.700
and the conclusion drawn from the premises

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:44.730
is what we refer to as inference.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.400
In this talk, he will
explore the mathematical

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.103
and philosophical aspects of inference,

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:54.240
and the word infer means to
deduce or draw a conclusion

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.940
from the evidence that's provided,

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.130
and it's the process of using logic

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:01.440
to come up with a conclusion.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.390
There are two types of inferences,

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:05.580
deductive and non-deductive inferences.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.900
So he will be delving into
the two kinds of inferences

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:13.950
and showing us the domain of validity

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:18.390
of these two forms of
inference, and in particular,

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:23.100
he is going to be showing
us that mathematics

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.970
has a very long tradition
of using formal reasoning,

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:30.810
but some mathematicians have
argued that there's a need

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.750
for non-deductive reasoning in the field.

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:37.470
So mathematics has always been
considered an exact science,

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:41.970
but in the last century, it
is seen as more of an art

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:46.590
with many different multiple
approaches for problems.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:49.320
So in today's talk,
Professor Paseau will present

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:52.080
some of his ideas about
non-deductive reasoning

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.800
in mathematics and explain why,
contrary to popular belief,

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:57.630
it can be actually a source of knowledge

00:03:57.630 --> 00:03:59.400
as well as justified belief,

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:02.880
but before I turn over the floor to him,

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.403
let me give you a formal introduction.

