00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:03.750
- Thank you very much,
Priya, for that warm welcome.

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:05.310
I'm extremely pleased to be here

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:07.770
and to be able to discuss with you

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:11.490
and share with a broader
audience some ideas.

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:14.640
It's been 20 years since we
were part of a discussion group

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:16.410
just out of grad school

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:19.230
and it's very nice to sort of repeat that.

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:22.800
I'd also like to thank the funders too.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.500
So my topic today is inference

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:28.320
from a logical and
philosophical perspective.

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.350
I work primarily in the philosophy
of mathematics and logic,

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:33.690
so that is effectively my perspective

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:38.690
that I'll be describing, and
I'll introduce the topic,

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:43.620
give you some references,
and then focus on two topics.

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:46.200
First, the idea that logic is infinite,

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:51.060
and secondly on non-deductive
inference reasoning

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:54.360
in mathematics and maybe
speculate at the end

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.223
on the link between the two.

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:02.820
So let's begin by distinguishing,
as Priya already did,

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:05.100
just to follow up on that between logical

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:07.623
and non-logical inference.

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:11.970
So what is the difference between the two?

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:13.650
Well, let's have an example.

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:18.210
The first inference that I
wanna mention is the following.

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:22.170
All horses are ungulates,
all ungulates are mammals,

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:24.720
so all horses are mammals.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:27.570
Okay, now you, I hope,

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:31.833
will recognize this to be a
formal, a valid inference,

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:36.840
that is to say the conclusion
follows from the premises.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:38.460
If the premises are true,

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:42.120
then the conclusion also has to be true,

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:44.550
and the interesting thing
about this inference

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:48.870
is it instantiates a formal
pattern, a formal schema.

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:53.070
All As are Bs, all Bs
are Cs, so all As are Cs,

00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:55.620
and you can see that any
instance of that is valid,

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:58.440
and in fact, in order to recognize

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:00.150
the validity of the argument,

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:04.490
you don't have to know what any
of the adjectives there mean

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:06.900
or any of the words like horses

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:09.210
and ungulates and mammals mean.

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:11.943
I try this out regularly
on my first-year students.

00:08:13.706 --> 00:08:15.960
I show them this argument and I ask them

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:19.080
whether the premises
make the conclusion true,

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:20.160
or if the premises are true,

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:22.230
does that mean that the
conclusion has to be true?

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:24.780
And they all recognize
that the argument is valid.

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:27.150
They all recognize that
the truth of the premises

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:31.350
necessitates the truth of
the conclusion, even though,

00:08:31.350 --> 00:08:33.450
and then I've asked them
a follow-up question,

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:35.010
even though most of them do not know

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:37.020
what the word ungulate means.

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:38.220
That just highlights, I think,

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:41.130
the fact that we can recognize
this argument to be valid

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:42.480
just in virtue of the form,

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:44.583
whatever the word ungulate means.

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:47.550
The counterpart to logical inference

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:49.290
is non-logical inference.

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:51.723
So just to give us a
simple example of this,

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:56.490
previous samples have boiled
at 100 degrees Celsius,

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:58.533
so this one also will.

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:01.050
So that's non-logical.

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:05.370
The conclusion does not
follow from the premise,

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:07.443
the single premise there necessarily,

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:09.903
and it's not formal.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:12.240
If you schematize it,

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:15.900
if you turn it into formal letters there,

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:19.710
you say previous As have been
Bs, so the next A will be a B.

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:22.860
You can see that that can be
a good inference in some cases

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:26.580
but will not invariably
be a good inference.

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:30.720
For example, if you've rolled
a die a couple of times

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:32.700
and it's been a six both times,

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:34.950
then to infer that it will be a six

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:36.400
the next time you throw it

00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:39.270
has a one in six chance of being true,

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:42.180
so certainly not a valid argument.

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:43.350
Okay, so that's the difference

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:46.410
between logical and non-logical inference.

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:49.020
How would we characterize
logical inference?

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:50.910
Many would define it as follows:

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:54.510
necessary truth-preservation
in virtue of form.

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:58.680
The conclusion follows
necessarily from the premises

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.983
in virtue of the form, as
in the ungulates argument,

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:04.530
and a mathematical proof,

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:07.500
and I'm gonna focus on mathematics later,

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:09.540
so it's a particular interest to me,

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:12.090
a mathematical proof is a
logically valid argument

00:10:12.090 --> 00:10:14.070
from some axioms,

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:16.230
and this is a fact that's
come to be appreciated

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:20.670
in the past 100, 120 years or so.

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:24.240
That's to say you can turn
any mathematical argument

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:26.820
into a logical argument from the axioms

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:29.550
of that particular area of mathematics,

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:32.853
or perhaps more broadly, the
axioms of all of mathematics,

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:36.720
and reasoning in science and everyday life

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:39.780
and also in mathematics has both logical

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:41.340
and non-logical aspects.

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:44.280
Sometimes we don't notice
it in everyday life,

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:47.610
but we do use logically valid arguments,

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:51.960
but we also often argue
in a non-logical way.

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:54.900
However, mathematics
is somewhat exceptional

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:58.470
in that there a claim is
regarded as established

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:00.060
only if it has been proved.

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:02.820
It's only a result if it's been proved.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:07.320
In other spheres of intellectual activity

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:10.050
in other walks of life,
you can establish a result,

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:12.900
a claim without proving
it in a strict sense,

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:15.630
but in mathematics a
claim is only regarded

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:17.637
as really established if it's been proved

00:11:17.637 --> 00:11:19.083
and it becomes a theorem.

00:11:24.165 --> 00:11:27.477
So I wanna talk about
both forms of validity

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:33.183
and my claim about logic
and stake out two claims,

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:36.480
one per form of validity.

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:38.140
The claim about logical validity

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:44.190
that I want to examine is
whether logic is finite.

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:46.573
Okay, and this is actually a sort of

00:11:46.573 --> 00:11:49.380
a tenet of contemporary thought,

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:52.560
or to put it sort of
pejoratively, a dogma,

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:56.190
and I will argue instead
that logical validity

00:11:56.190 --> 00:11:57.060
can be infinite.

00:11:57.060 --> 00:11:59.700
What it means for logic
to be finite or infinite

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:01.533
I will clarify shortly.

00:12:03.210 --> 00:12:07.860
The other sort of tenet is
about non-logical validity,

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:09.630
is, in mathematics at least,

00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:11.280
that this is sort of second rate.

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:16.410
The best form of argument
is, as I mentioned,

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:19.710
logical or deductive you might say,

00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:23.460
and in mathematics, anything
that doesn't meet that standard

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:27.210
in some sense doesn't cut the
mustard, and so in particular,

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:29.520
a non-logical inference
cannot provide knowledge

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:30.723
of a mathematical claim.

00:12:32.460 --> 00:12:34.590
Okay, so those are the two top,

00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:37.290
the two theses I'm going to talk about

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:39.330
and try to argue against,

00:12:39.330 --> 00:12:43.890
giving some reasons for my views.

00:12:43.890 --> 00:12:45.510
Gonna give you some references here

00:12:45.510 --> 00:12:48.810
on the logical validity side of things.

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:50.970
This idea that logic is infinite

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:53.973
that I'll be describing and defending,

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:57.220
there are some pioneers in the field

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:00.420
on whose work I'm building.

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:02.940
The first is Alfred Tarski,

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:07.080
who in a 1966 lecture that
was posthumously published

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:11.822
in 1986 first developed the
idea that logic is infinite,

00:13:11.822 --> 00:13:14.733
to put it broadly speaking,
and then there's Gila Sher,

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:18.240
who I'm very pleased is with us today

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:21.180
and who will be my discussant tomorrow,

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:24.120
and in her work building on Tarski,

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:28.417
she has also developed this
idea ranging from an early book,

00:13:28.417 --> 00:13:31.170
"The Bounds of Logic" in
'91 to her most recent book,

00:13:31.170 --> 00:13:34.650
very recent, in fact,
"Logical Consequence,"

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:38.070
and my contribution to this debate,

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:40.860
to this domain has been most recently

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:44.100
in the monograph "One True Logic"

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:45.930
co-written with Owen Griffiths,

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:47.730
which came out a few months ago.

00:13:47.730 --> 00:13:49.650
That's on the logical validity side,

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:52.800
and then the sort of references on the,

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:54.720
if you wanna do some
further reading on this,

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:57.930
on the non-logical
reasoning side of things,

00:13:57.930 --> 00:14:00.870
there's a classic mid
20th century classics

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:02.917
classic by George Polya called

00:14:02.917 --> 00:14:05.820
"Mathematics and Plausible Reasoning,"

00:14:05.820 --> 00:14:09.990
and that came out in the '50s and '60s.

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:12.390
More recent and more
philosophical engagement

00:14:12.390 --> 00:14:16.890
has been by Don Fallis
in a couple of articles,

00:14:16.890 --> 00:14:21.000
and I've cited one of mine from 2015.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:22.260
Okay, so those are the references

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:25.050
if anyone wants to do
some follow-up reading.

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:27.240
So let's move to the first big idea

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:31.590
that logic is not, as sort
of current thinking has it,

00:14:31.590 --> 00:14:33.750
finite but is in fact infinite.

00:14:33.750 --> 00:14:36.720
Okay, so the first thing
we need to get clear on is

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:39.900
what it means for logic
to be finite or infinite.

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:43.320
Okay, so a logic is in many ways

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:45.090
like an ordinary language, right?

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:48.060
Such as pick your favorite
ordinary natural language,

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:51.453
ordinary language, English,
Arabic, Spanish, Mandarin.

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:54.780
Philosophers, by the
way, as I already did,

00:14:54.780 --> 00:14:59.780
call these natural languages
in distinction to logic.

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:04.980
The language of a logic
contains some vocabulary

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:06.570
and it also contains rules

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:08.670
for stringing together the vocabulary.

00:15:08.670 --> 00:15:09.870
Call that a grammar.

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:13.290
That's true of a natural
language such as English as well.

00:15:13.290 --> 00:15:18.150
So let's give two examples of
grammatical rules for English.

00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:20.310
So if A and B are sentences,

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:24.090
then their conjunction,
A and B, is a sentence.

00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:26.940
So for example, if "Fido
is a dog" is a sentence

00:15:26.940 --> 00:15:29.190
and "Felix is a cat" is a sentence,

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:33.120
then so is the sentence "Fido
a dog and Felix is a cat."

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:35.100
That's called their disjunction,

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:37.890
and if A and B are sentences,
then so is their disjunction.

00:15:37.890 --> 00:15:40.683
That's when you join them
together by the word or,

00:15:42.330 --> 00:15:44.064
and to say that that logic is finite,

00:15:44.064 --> 00:15:45.210
just to put it very roughly,

00:15:45.210 --> 00:15:48.213
is to say that all the
sentences are of finite length,

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:51.630
but in contrast, an infinite
logic allows you to conjoin,

00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:53.393
join with and, or disjoin, join with or,

00:15:55.260 --> 00:15:58.140
infinitely many sentences
and to quantify over

00:15:58.140 --> 00:15:59.970
infinitely many variables.

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:03.870
So you can say there
exists not just one X,

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:06.090
there exists X, there
exists Y, there exists Z,

00:16:06.090 --> 00:16:08.793
but you can quantify over
infinitely many variables.

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:13.290
Okay, and I'll explain that
a bit more as we go along.

00:16:13.290 --> 00:16:15.270
Infinite logics have been a minor area

00:16:15.270 --> 00:16:18.180
of mathematical study since the 1950s,

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:20.130
but they're not generally
regarded as correct,

00:16:20.130 --> 00:16:24.180
as capturing correct form of implication.

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:25.590
The usual term here, by the way,

00:16:25.590 --> 00:16:28.200
in the field is finitary
versus infinitary,

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:30.600
but for the purposes of this talk,

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:33.993
I'm gonna just use the
simpler finite and infinite.

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:39.690
So the argument for the infinity of logic

00:16:39.690 --> 00:16:43.380
is to my mind made up of
two sorts of considerations.

00:16:43.380 --> 00:16:45.720
There's what I call the
bottom-up arguments,

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:50.720
and these rest on relatively
light theoretical principles.

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:53.430
You don't need a great deal of theory

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:55.800
or theoretical commitments
to accept these.

00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:57.480
They sort of rest on things
that most people believe

00:16:57.480 --> 00:17:01.983
about logic already or
slight extensions of them.

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:05.250
The top-down arguments
are much more theoretical.

00:17:05.250 --> 00:17:06.930
They're based on the nature of logic

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:09.780
and/or the logical constants.

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:10.860
What are the logical constants?

00:17:10.860 --> 00:17:14.730
That won't be that relevant
today, but they are,

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:16.350
just to clarify that they're expressions

00:17:16.350 --> 00:17:20.310
such as and or there exists,
and those are dealt with,

00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:21.870
the two sorts of arguments are discussed

00:17:21.870 --> 00:17:24.603
in respective chapters
of my recent monograph.

00:17:25.710 --> 00:17:27.783
Okay, so let's look at an argument.

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:31.950
Let's consider this following argument.

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:34.860
It consists of the premise
there is at least one planet,

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:38.400
a second premise, there
are at least two planets,

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:39.960
third premise, there are
at least three planets,

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:41.853
and so on for all finite numbers.

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:44.277
So it has infinitely many premises

00:17:44.277 --> 00:17:45.600
and then it has a conclusion

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:48.900
and that's drawn below the line.

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:51.690
The conclusion is there are
infinitely many planets.

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:54.303
Okay, so if we think about that argument,

00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:58.170
this argument seems valid in some sense.

00:17:58.170 --> 00:18:03.170
That's to say the premises
necessitate the conclusion, okay?

00:18:04.170 --> 00:18:05.700
If the premises, or put it another way,

00:18:05.700 --> 00:18:07.650
if the premises are true,
the conclusion must be true,

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:09.420
because if there's at least
one planet, at least two,

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:10.893
at least three, at least four and so on,

00:18:10.893 --> 00:18:13.170
then there's gotta be
infinitely many, okay?

00:18:13.170 --> 00:18:17.370
And the question is whether
it's logically valid or not.

00:18:17.370 --> 00:18:19.860
Okay, so my claim, which I will
not be able to defend here,

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:20.820
but I will just state,

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:22.950
is that this argument is
in fact logically valid.

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:24.480
One can show that it's logically valid

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:26.520
or one can argue that it is.

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:29.340
The second claim is that standard logic,

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:32.520
which is finite, cannot capture it, okay?

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:35.190
And this is actually quite
an easy claim to establish,

00:18:35.190 --> 00:18:38.520
that the standard logic,
which is first-order logic,

00:18:38.520 --> 00:18:41.643
cannot capture this
argument as logically valid,

00:18:42.510 --> 00:18:44.520
because, briefly,

00:18:44.520 --> 00:18:47.043
if in first-order logic,
in standard logic,

00:18:48.391 --> 00:18:50.790
if there's an argument with
infinitely many premises

00:18:50.790 --> 00:18:52.223
and a conclusion, it's a valid argument,

00:18:52.223 --> 00:18:54.750
there's also gotta be a sub-argument

00:18:54.750 --> 00:18:57.330
made up of finitely many of those premises

00:18:57.330 --> 00:18:59.160
that entails the conclusion,

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:02.310
and clearly, if you just have
finite many of those premises,

00:19:02.310 --> 00:19:03.990
you know that there are, say, two planets,

00:19:03.990 --> 00:19:05.640
at least two planets, at least 53,

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:08.880
at least 412 up to some finite number,

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:11.970
then it doesn't follow that
there are infinitely many.

00:19:11.970 --> 00:19:14.280
The third claim is that
other finite logics

00:19:14.280 --> 00:19:16.923
cannot capture this
argument's generalizations,

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:21.240
and the fourth claim is
that an infinite logic can.

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:24.930
So this is what I call a
bottom-up argument, right?

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:27.540
In order to capture the
validity of this argument,

00:19:27.540 --> 00:19:29.940
assuming it is logically
valid, and that's claim one,

00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:32.860
you need effectively what the argument is

00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:37.200
is that you need a infinite logic.

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:38.970
A finite logic isn't gonna cut it.

00:19:38.970 --> 00:19:40.410
It's not gonna be able to capture

00:19:40.410 --> 00:19:42.540
the validity of this argument.

00:19:42.540 --> 00:19:44.920
Let me give another example of

00:19:46.170 --> 00:19:49.353
another consideration on
the sort of bottom-up side.

00:19:50.550 --> 00:19:52.800
Consider this argument here,

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:54.540
and the reason I wanna
give another argument

00:19:54.540 --> 00:19:57.390
is to make it look a little
bit less mathematical,

00:19:57.390 --> 00:19:58.653
less to do with numbers.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:02.520
Bob is not my parent. Bob
is not my grandparent.

00:20:02.520 --> 00:20:05.220
Bob is not my great
grandparent and so on, okay?

00:20:05.220 --> 00:20:07.080
You keep on iterating the greats.

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:08.853
Therefore, Bob is not my ancestor.

00:20:10.020 --> 00:20:11.910
Now, this argument is not formally valid.

00:20:11.910 --> 00:20:14.340
If you change the word
parent in all the premises,

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:17.580
so in the word in the first premise,

00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:19.083
turn it into, say, child,

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:23.190
turn the word parent in the second premise

00:20:23.190 --> 00:20:25.620
to child to make it grandchild and so on,

00:20:25.620 --> 00:20:28.080
then the conclusion will not follow.

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:29.640
So it's not a formally valid argument.

00:20:29.640 --> 00:20:32.520
It's not just valid in virtue of form,

00:20:32.520 --> 00:20:35.193
but if we add the meaning premise,

00:20:36.180 --> 00:20:38.010
we can make the argument formally valid,

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:40.440
and the meaning premise
here is the premise

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:42.870
about the meaning of the word ancestor,

00:20:42.870 --> 00:20:44.310
and what's the relevant idea?

00:20:44.310 --> 00:20:46.890
Well, you're my ancestor
if you are my parent

00:20:46.890 --> 00:20:49.350
or grandparent or
great-grandparent or so on, okay?

00:20:49.350 --> 00:20:52.503
So this is a list that
continues ad infinitum,

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:55.530
and if we add that premise,

00:20:55.530 --> 00:20:58.350
then the argument becomes formally valid

00:20:58.350 --> 00:21:01.980
and can be captured in an infinite logic.

00:21:01.980 --> 00:21:03.093
So just to give you a sense of

00:21:03.093 --> 00:21:05.130
what an infinite logic looks like,

00:21:05.130 --> 00:21:07.773
the sentences of an
infinite logic look like,

00:21:08.610 --> 00:21:10.590
the formalization of this Bob argument

00:21:10.590 --> 00:21:12.890
plus the meaning premise
would look like this.

00:21:13.930 --> 00:21:16.397
The first premise would
get formalized as not P1b.

00:21:16.397 --> 00:21:20.100
That's to be understood as
Bob is not my parent, right?

00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:23.370
So the first symbol there is a not sign,

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:28.203
P1 means is my parent, and
b can be understood as Bob,

00:21:29.100 --> 00:21:30.930
not Bob, the second premise,

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:33.420
Bob is not my grandparent and so on,

00:21:33.420 --> 00:21:36.630
and the meaning premise which
we've added is that for all x,

00:21:36.630 --> 00:21:39.750
x is an ancestor if and only if,

00:21:39.750 --> 00:21:42.493
that double arrow can be
read as if and only if,

00:21:44.100 --> 00:21:47.040
some, that thing, that
x is either my parent

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:49.410
or my grandparent or my
great-grandparent and so on,

00:21:49.410 --> 00:21:53.760
and notice that that big V
sign, it's a big disjunction,

00:21:53.760 --> 00:21:55.080
is infinite, right?

00:21:55.080 --> 00:21:59.910
You need to disjoin infinitely
many formulas there.

00:21:59.910 --> 00:22:02.571
So that's the sense in which that formula,

00:22:02.571 --> 00:22:04.230
that logic is infinite, and from that,

00:22:04.230 --> 00:22:06.780
the conclusion follows that
Bob is not my ancestor.

00:22:08.130 --> 00:22:11.460
Okay, so that's the,

00:22:11.460 --> 00:22:13.920
these are the sorts of bottom-up arguments

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:17.340
that one can develop further

00:22:17.340 --> 00:22:19.473
to argue that logic should be infinite.

00:22:20.790 --> 00:22:22.620
Another sort of bottom-up argument,

00:22:22.620 --> 00:22:24.630
it consists of a sort of-

00:22:24.630 --> 00:22:26.550
- [Priya] Alex, I have a quick question.

00:22:26.550 --> 00:22:27.840
- [Alex] Yes.

00:22:27.840 --> 00:22:30.690
- [Priya] So that rests on the fact

00:22:30.690 --> 00:22:32.970
that you have an infinite layered

00:22:32.970 --> 00:22:34.593
set of relationships, right?

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:38.040
- You have an infinite
premise set, correct.

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:39.660
- [Priya] Right. Okay.

00:22:39.660 --> 00:22:42.210
- Yeah, actually, that's
very relevant to something

00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:45.570
that I'll mention in a
couple of, in a few slides.

00:22:45.570 --> 00:22:46.420
Thank you, Priya.

00:22:48.630 --> 00:22:50.940
So the thought experiment

00:22:50.940 --> 00:22:53.070
imagines the following sort of being,

00:22:53.070 --> 00:22:56.760
a superhuman who can
accomplish supertasks.

00:22:56.760 --> 00:22:58.950
These are infinite tasks
that can be completed

00:22:58.950 --> 00:23:01.620
in a finite amount of time, okay?

00:23:01.620 --> 00:23:05.160
So a superhuman is capable of uttering

00:23:05.160 --> 00:23:07.110
an infinitely long sentence,
such as the following,

00:23:07.110 --> 00:23:11.250
0 is even and 2 is even
and 4 is even and so on.

00:23:11.250 --> 00:23:12.693
So for every even number,

00:23:14.717 --> 00:23:16.440
that claim that that number is even

00:23:16.440 --> 00:23:18.540
gets included in the sentence.

00:23:18.540 --> 00:23:20.190
How does a superhuman do it?

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:23.520
Well, they utter 0 is even in one second,

00:23:23.520 --> 00:23:24.720
then they speed up.

00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:26.940
They say n2 is even in 1/2 a second.

00:23:26.940 --> 00:23:30.510
They say n4 is even in 1/4
of a second, and so on,

00:23:30.510 --> 00:23:31.830
so that after two seconds,

00:23:31.830 --> 00:23:34.680
they've uttered an infinitely
long sentence, okay?

00:23:34.680 --> 00:23:37.803
Now, superhumans, we do not think exist.

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:40.820
They're not biologically
possible, perhaps.

00:23:41.670 --> 00:23:43.890
Perhaps they're physically
possible, perhaps they're not.

00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:45.030
The important point here is

00:23:45.030 --> 00:23:47.400
that they're certainly
conceptually possible.

00:23:47.400 --> 00:23:49.740
There's nothing sort of conceptually wrong

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:51.840
with assuming that they could exist,

00:23:51.840 --> 00:23:53.643
even if they don't actually exist.

00:23:54.720 --> 00:23:56.490
So suppose that superhumans perform

00:23:56.490 --> 00:23:58.320
the following sort of inference.

00:23:58.320 --> 00:24:00.310
They utter this infinitely long premise

00:24:01.170 --> 00:24:04.800
and then they utter another
infinitely long sentence,

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:07.530
again using speed up, but
this one's a little different.

00:24:07.530 --> 00:24:12.150
They say zero is even, n4 is
even, n8 is even, and so on.

00:24:12.150 --> 00:24:14.130
So what they do in uttering the conclusion

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:19.130
is they drop every other
clause in the premise.

00:24:19.710 --> 00:24:21.720
They drop the 2 is even clause

00:24:21.720 --> 00:24:23.790
and 6 is even clause
and 10 is even and so on

00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:26.763
and they just say the multiples of 4.

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:31.680
Okay, so I think, so
this looks very much like

00:24:31.680 --> 00:24:34.050
a sort of inference that logicians think

00:24:34.050 --> 00:24:36.540
is completely fine in the finite case.

00:24:36.540 --> 00:24:37.950
You know, if you have A and B

00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:40.830
and from that you infer that A,

00:24:40.830 --> 00:24:43.650
clearly that's completely
fine and that's valid,

00:24:43.650 --> 00:24:44.940
but in this case there's a twist

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:47.490
because we have an infinitely long premise

00:24:47.490 --> 00:24:49.190
and an infinitely long conclusion.

00:24:50.610 --> 00:24:51.900
So it's an infinite form of

00:24:51.900 --> 00:24:54.603
what logicians call
conjunction elimination.

00:24:55.799 --> 00:24:57.570
You have a conjunction and you eliminate

00:24:57.570 --> 00:25:01.650
some of the conjuncts and
this looks just as valid

00:25:01.650 --> 00:25:05.673
as a finite form of
conjunction elimination, okay?

00:25:06.990 --> 00:25:08.977
And you might say in response to this,

00:25:08.977 --> 00:25:11.370
"Well, this inference is something

00:25:11.370 --> 00:25:13.560
that we can describe in finite terms."

00:25:13.560 --> 00:25:15.960
I've just done this in
giving you the slide

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:17.790
and talking about it, but actually,

00:25:17.790 --> 00:25:19.350
we've gotta imagine the superhumans

00:25:19.350 --> 00:25:23.160
actually carrying out the inference, okay?

00:25:23.160 --> 00:25:25.140
We can come back to that in
the questions if you like,

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:28.440
but that's the sort of thought experiment

00:25:28.440 --> 00:25:30.750
that is supposed to
make more vivid the idea

00:25:30.750 --> 00:25:33.930
that if finite logic is,

00:25:33.930 --> 00:25:36.060
a standard finite logic is logic,

00:25:36.060 --> 00:25:39.540
then why not go further and think

00:25:39.540 --> 00:25:42.333
that an infinite logic is also logic?

00:25:43.710 --> 00:25:48.000
Okay, so there are more bottom-up
arguments than the above,

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:50.490
but it's time I think for a
sort of important clarification,

00:25:50.490 --> 00:25:53.250
and here I must confess to something.

00:25:53.250 --> 00:25:54.870
I have to fess up to something.

00:25:54.870 --> 00:25:57.360
I labeled my talk

00:25:57.360 --> 00:26:00.540
Inference: A
Logical-Philosophical Perspective,

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:02.340
but actually in this part of the talk,

00:26:02.340 --> 00:26:05.970
I'm talking about implication
rather than inference,

00:26:05.970 --> 00:26:08.580
strictly speaking, and
what is the difference?

00:26:08.580 --> 00:26:11.970
Implication is really a relation
between sentences, okay?

00:26:11.970 --> 00:26:15.660
Something that holds from
between some premises

00:26:15.660 --> 00:26:17.520
and a conclusion or premise set,

00:26:17.520 --> 00:26:19.050
if you like, and a conclusion,

00:26:19.050 --> 00:26:21.150
and it's a fact that
holds between sentences

00:26:21.150 --> 00:26:22.830
and it's not anthropocentric.

00:26:22.830 --> 00:26:24.300
So in the ungulates argument,

00:26:24.300 --> 00:26:26.130
whether the premises entail the conclusion

00:26:26.130 --> 00:26:28.110
is not something that we do.

00:26:28.110 --> 00:26:30.510
It's just the fact about those sentences.

00:26:30.510 --> 00:26:31.710
Inference, on the other hand,

00:26:31.710 --> 00:26:34.980
is something that an agent performs, okay?

00:26:34.980 --> 00:26:37.170
And it is an anthropocentric notion

00:26:37.170 --> 00:26:41.493
because an agent reasons from
premises to a conclusion.

00:26:42.840 --> 00:26:44.790
Even if we consider idealized agents,

00:26:44.790 --> 00:26:48.570
that's still a sort of
anthropocentric notion, okay?

00:26:48.570 --> 00:26:52.140
Even, for example, if we think
about Bayesian epistemology,

00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:54.720
about Bayesian reasoners who are idealized

00:26:54.720 --> 00:26:57.090
in some sense, in some ways,

00:26:57.090 --> 00:26:59.670
that still is a form of inference,

00:26:59.670 --> 00:27:02.910
and logic's main role is, I think,

00:27:02.910 --> 00:27:04.500
I believe, to capture implication.

00:27:04.500 --> 00:27:07.023
It captures the relations
between sentences.

00:27:08.610 --> 00:27:11.250
How does it relate to inference?

00:27:11.250 --> 00:27:13.740
Well, the study of
inference and rationality

00:27:13.740 --> 00:27:17.613
is informed by logic, but it
doesn't boil down to logic.

00:27:18.540 --> 00:27:20.670
So let me give you an example
of what I mean by that.

00:27:20.670 --> 00:27:25.670
So suppose you believe A, some
fact A, and you ask yourself,

00:27:25.687 --> 00:27:28.830
"What else should I think if I believe A?"

00:27:28.830 --> 00:27:31.470
Well, then you might come to appreciate

00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:33.570
the fact that A logically
implies B, right?

00:27:33.570 --> 00:27:37.143
This is a fact about
sentences, that A implies B,

00:27:38.190 --> 00:27:41.170
but whether you then go on to believe B

00:27:42.150 --> 00:27:45.077
isn't determined entirely by the fact

00:27:45.077 --> 00:27:47.040
that A logically implies B.

00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:50.007
You may think that B, you
may find B very implausible,

00:27:50.007 --> 00:27:52.440
and you may think, "Hang on, I believed A.

00:27:53.400 --> 00:27:56.340
I now realize that A implies
B, but B is very implausible,

00:27:56.340 --> 00:27:58.320
so maybe I should have
another think about A.

00:27:58.320 --> 00:28:00.780
Maybe I should not believe A," okay?

00:28:00.780 --> 00:28:04.015
So there may be all sorts of rational ways

00:28:04.015 --> 00:28:06.390
to react to implication facts

00:28:06.390 --> 00:28:08.590
and implication facts
aren't gonna determine

00:28:09.480 --> 00:28:10.800
all inferential facts.

00:28:10.800 --> 00:28:14.580
So implication captures
facts about sentences,

00:28:14.580 --> 00:28:18.663
and inferences, facts
about what reasoners do.

00:28:20.520 --> 00:28:22.803
Okay, so having said that,

00:28:24.030 --> 00:28:27.120
we can ask what language
applied logicians or logicians

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:31.560
who think about using logic
to capture reasonings,

00:28:31.560 --> 00:28:33.340
as in the to capture

00:28:35.760 --> 00:28:37.470
our arguments couched in language,

00:28:37.470 --> 00:28:40.650
such as the ungulates argument
or the planets argument

00:28:40.650 --> 00:28:42.900
or the Bob argument I gave earlier,

00:28:42.900 --> 00:28:45.630
what language are we
actually interested in here?

00:28:45.630 --> 00:28:47.703
So I've given these arguments in English,

00:28:48.630 --> 00:28:50.850
but actually, the choice
of a particular language,

00:28:50.850 --> 00:28:53.160
such as English, for
example, a natural language,

00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:54.870
even if you understand a language

00:28:54.870 --> 00:28:57.030
to include all the
technical vocabulary, right?

00:28:57.030 --> 00:28:59.250
So when I say the
examples I've given so far

00:28:59.250 --> 00:29:03.540
have mostly been sort of
homely bits of vocabulary,

00:29:03.540 --> 00:29:06.810
you know, about planets,
about horses and mammals,

00:29:06.810 --> 00:29:10.650
about Bob being my ancestor
or not, that sort of thing,

00:29:10.650 --> 00:29:13.220
but you can throw in all the
sort of technical vocabulary

00:29:13.220 --> 00:29:15.120
of the sciences, of mathematics,

00:29:15.120 --> 00:29:17.700
of the law, and so on, so on,

00:29:17.700 --> 00:29:20.460
but the choice of any
such language would be,

00:29:20.460 --> 00:29:22.290
as the sort of focus,

00:29:22.290 --> 00:29:25.710
this central focus of a logician's study,

00:29:25.710 --> 00:29:27.213
would be somewhat parochial.

00:29:28.380 --> 00:29:32.040
Why is that? Well, because
natural languages sort of grow.

00:29:32.040 --> 00:29:34.230
They accrue words by the day.

00:29:34.230 --> 00:29:38.760
Just to take an example, "I
can't play my DVD on my laptop."

00:29:38.760 --> 00:29:41.220
Well, that would've been
incomprehensible to Victorians.

00:29:41.220 --> 00:29:44.100
Okay, so it would be quite
odd to give an account

00:29:44.100 --> 00:29:48.480
of logical implication that
just applied to English

00:29:48.480 --> 00:29:53.133
as it is today on the
28th of September, 2022.

00:29:54.330 --> 00:29:57.453
That would seem too parochial,
too narrow a project.

00:29:59.400 --> 00:30:01.020
The logician should really be interested

00:30:01.020 --> 00:30:03.600
not in any particular natural language,

00:30:03.600 --> 00:30:04.770
including the technical parts,

00:30:04.770 --> 00:30:09.480
as it is today but in
its extensions as well,

00:30:09.480 --> 00:30:11.160
and once we appreciate this fact,

00:30:11.160 --> 00:30:13.320
that opens the door to generalizations

00:30:13.320 --> 00:30:14.940
of the bottom-up arguments

00:30:14.940 --> 00:30:16.755
that go beyond the countably infinite,

00:30:16.755 --> 00:30:19.590
that's to say that go beyond the infinity

00:30:19.590 --> 00:30:23.550
of the natural numbers
0, 1, 2, 3, and so on.

00:30:23.550 --> 00:30:26.700
Okay, so that's so much for
the bottom-up arguments.

00:30:26.700 --> 00:30:30.000
So they strongly point to the conclusion

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:33.930
that logic cannot be finite
but must be infinite.

00:30:33.930 --> 00:30:38.430
Now let's move on to the
other type of argument

00:30:38.430 --> 00:30:42.210
for the infinity of logic and
this is a top-down argument

00:30:42.210 --> 00:30:44.220
about the nature of logic.

00:30:44.220 --> 00:30:45.540
So what is the nature of logic?

00:30:45.540 --> 00:30:50.250
Well, two features of logic
that are generally accepted

00:30:50.250 --> 00:30:52.830
are generality and topic neutrality.

00:30:52.830 --> 00:30:56.430
Generality is the idea that
logic applies to all spheres,

00:30:56.430 --> 00:31:00.090
and topic neutrality, a
very closely related idea,

00:31:00.090 --> 00:31:02.700
is that it doesn't have a topic of its own

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:06.300
but rather it applies to all areas.

00:31:06.300 --> 00:31:10.890
So for example, mathematics
is about numbers

00:31:10.890 --> 00:31:15.890
and functions and shapes in geometry,

00:31:16.080 --> 00:31:19.740
and physics might study particles,

00:31:19.740 --> 00:31:23.160
economics, rational economic behavior,

00:31:23.160 --> 00:31:26.550
be it of idealized agents or
actual economies and so on.

00:31:26.550 --> 00:31:27.960
These all have topics,

00:31:27.960 --> 00:31:30.813
but logic is topic neutral is the idea,

00:31:31.860 --> 00:31:34.140
and the logician that I mentioned earlier,

00:31:34.140 --> 00:31:36.810
famous 20th century logician Tarski,

00:31:36.810 --> 00:31:39.330
he made this idea of
topic neutrality precise

00:31:39.330 --> 00:31:40.983
by considering permutations.

00:31:41.820 --> 00:31:44.430
So if you look, consider the set A, B, C

00:31:44.430 --> 00:31:47.553
and a permutation mapping
A to B, B to C, and C to A,

00:31:49.020 --> 00:31:50.760
think of that permutation.

00:31:50.760 --> 00:31:53.280
It's just shuffling the elements around.

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:55.980
Well, some sets, such as the set A, B, C,

00:31:55.980 --> 00:32:00.420
is mapped to itself by
this permutation, okay?

00:32:00.420 --> 00:32:02.430
Some sets are mapped to
themselves by the permutation.

00:32:02.430 --> 00:32:06.090
They're invariant under it
because A gets mapped to B,

00:32:06.090 --> 00:32:08.340
B to C, C to A, but others are not.

00:32:08.340 --> 00:32:11.203
For example, A, B is mapped to the other,

00:32:11.203 --> 00:32:12.750
a distinct set, B and C,

00:32:12.750 --> 00:32:15.330
because A gets mapped to B and B to C.

00:32:15.330 --> 00:32:17.940
So it's variant under the permutation,

00:32:17.940 --> 00:32:20.220
and if you think of properties
and relations as sets

00:32:20.220 --> 00:32:22.200
and define what it is for
a property or relation

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:24.990
to be permutation invariant,

00:32:24.990 --> 00:32:29.370
then you can get a
notion applying to these

00:32:29.370 --> 00:32:32.250
of variants applying to these
properties or relations,

00:32:32.250 --> 00:32:34.500
so for example, the
property being American

00:32:34.500 --> 00:32:36.570
and the property being self-identical.

00:32:36.570 --> 00:32:39.780
If you think about all the eight billion,

00:32:39.780 --> 00:32:43.525
almost eight billion
people on the planet today,

00:32:43.525 --> 00:32:46.410
a small subset of those are American.

00:32:46.410 --> 00:32:50.260
So if you permute the eight billion people

00:32:51.630 --> 00:32:53.370
on the planet today and you map

00:32:53.370 --> 00:32:55.470
some Americans to some non-Americans,

00:32:55.470 --> 00:32:58.713
then the property the set
of the Americans changes.

00:33:00.330 --> 00:33:02.490
However, the property
of being self-identical

00:33:02.490 --> 00:33:04.920
is a property that's had
by absolutely everyone

00:33:04.920 --> 00:33:06.270
on the planet, okay?

00:33:06.270 --> 00:33:09.030
So however you permute
the people on the planet,

00:33:09.030 --> 00:33:10.680
the set of people on the planet,

00:33:10.680 --> 00:33:12.090
that property is not gonna change.

00:33:12.090 --> 00:33:14.943
It's gonna be the set of all people, okay?

00:33:16.265 --> 00:33:19.110
And that aligns with sort
of intuitive judgments

00:33:19.110 --> 00:33:21.420
that being American is
not a logical property,

00:33:21.420 --> 00:33:24.183
but being self-identical
is a logical property.

00:33:26.400 --> 00:33:31.170
Now, there's a complication
here because being human,

00:33:31.170 --> 00:33:33.720
for example, on the domain
of all people alive today,

00:33:33.720 --> 00:33:35.790
as I said, the almost
eight billion people,

00:33:35.790 --> 00:33:38.820
well, that will be
permutation invariant as well,

00:33:38.820 --> 00:33:41.340
because however you permute
them, they're all human,

00:33:41.340 --> 00:33:45.150
so the set of human beings over this

00:33:45.150 --> 00:33:49.353
is gonna stay fixed over
the world's population,

00:33:50.820 --> 00:33:54.840
but like being American, being
human is not topic neutral.

00:33:54.840 --> 00:33:59.840
So Gila Sher had the
idea, the correct idea,

00:33:59.880 --> 00:34:02.490
that to remove all
sensitivity to subject matter,

00:34:02.490 --> 00:34:05.370
we must consider not just
permutations of a domain

00:34:05.370 --> 00:34:07.920
but bijections to others of the same size.

00:34:07.920 --> 00:34:11.700
That's to say you map them to
other sets of the same size.

00:34:11.700 --> 00:34:14.013
So you've got the almost
eight billion people,

00:34:14.970 --> 00:34:19.920
the domain of all of the
inhabitants of the planet today.

00:34:19.920 --> 00:34:21.810
You can map those almost
eight billion people to,

00:34:21.810 --> 00:34:24.120
say, the natural numbers,

00:34:24.120 --> 00:34:28.990
and then being human is not
preserved under that mapping,

00:34:30.150 --> 00:34:31.770
and the technical name for this,

00:34:31.770 --> 00:34:35.790
which I will not use but
I want to say nonetheless,

00:34:35.790 --> 00:34:38.273
is isomorphism invariance,

00:34:38.273 --> 00:34:41.250
and the theorem proved by
Vann McGee links this notion

00:34:41.250 --> 00:34:43.530
of invariance to the infinity of logic.

00:34:43.530 --> 00:34:46.020
So roughly a property or
relation of the right type

00:34:46.020 --> 00:34:49.797
is invariant just when it's
expressible by a formula of,

00:34:49.797 --> 00:34:53.250
and this is how this is
pronounced, L infinity infinity,

00:34:53.250 --> 00:34:55.320
and this logic, L infinity infinity,

00:34:55.320 --> 00:34:58.320
is in a sense as infinite as can be,

00:34:58.320 --> 00:35:00.123
highly, highly infinite logic.

00:35:00.960 --> 00:35:03.390
So a more technical explanation would be

00:35:03.390 --> 00:35:05.550
to say that it extends standard logic

00:35:05.550 --> 00:35:09.870
by allowing for any,
any, any cardinal kappa,

00:35:09.870 --> 00:35:10.890
it's the Greek letter kappa,

00:35:10.890 --> 00:35:14.034
conjunctions and disjunctions
of kappa-many disjuncts,

00:35:14.034 --> 00:35:16.200
conjuncts or disjuncts respectively

00:35:16.200 --> 00:35:19.743
and allowing quantification
over kappa-many argument places.

00:35:21.030 --> 00:35:23.100
So to properly appreciate this,

00:35:23.100 --> 00:35:24.180
you have to know that there are

00:35:24.180 --> 00:35:26.100
lots of different sizes of infinity,

00:35:26.100 --> 00:35:27.810
and what this is saying is actually

00:35:27.810 --> 00:35:31.920
for whatever size of
infinity you pick, you,

00:35:31.920 --> 00:35:34.950
the logic L infinity infinity is as large,

00:35:34.950 --> 00:35:36.363
at least as large as that.

00:35:37.560 --> 00:35:41.490
So just to, because this
was somewhat more technical,

00:35:41.490 --> 00:35:44.220
this last idea, the sort
of the top-down argument,

00:35:44.220 --> 00:35:45.393
I wanna summarize it.

00:35:46.380 --> 00:35:47.213
How does it start?

00:35:47.213 --> 00:35:49.470
It starts from the
characterization of logic

00:35:49.470 --> 00:35:52.083
as being topic neutral, okay?

00:35:53.280 --> 00:35:54.540
And topic neutrality,

00:35:54.540 --> 00:35:56.580
and this is something
that's generally agreed,

00:35:56.580 --> 00:35:59.400
topic neutrality can be
more precisely understood

00:35:59.400 --> 00:36:03.570
as the notion of invariance
that I described, okay?

00:36:03.570 --> 00:36:07.320
That's a more precise way of spelling out

00:36:07.320 --> 00:36:09.630
and it's a precise way of spelling out

00:36:09.630 --> 00:36:12.360
this notion of topic neutrality,

00:36:12.360 --> 00:36:15.480
and it turns out that this
notion of invariance coincides

00:36:15.480 --> 00:36:18.380
with what can be characterized
in a highly infinite logic.

00:36:19.890 --> 00:36:21.687
So the upshot is that we've
got a top-down argument

00:36:21.687 --> 00:36:22.860
for the infinity of logic.

00:36:22.860 --> 00:36:25.020
If you ask, "What is logic?"

00:36:25.020 --> 00:36:26.940
the answer is it's topic neutral.

00:36:26.940 --> 00:36:30.180
You spell that out and it
turns out, on that basis,

00:36:30.180 --> 00:36:31.470
you get an argument that logic

00:36:31.470 --> 00:36:33.543
is not just infinite but highly infinite,

00:36:35.310 --> 00:36:38.070
and so these two sorts of
arguments actually converge.

00:36:38.070 --> 00:36:40.020
They point to the same conclusion.

00:36:40.020 --> 00:36:42.660
The top-down and the bottom-up arguments

00:36:42.660 --> 00:36:45.870
point to the conclusion that
logic is highly infinite,

00:36:45.870 --> 00:36:48.660
which in today's climate
is quite a radical thought

00:36:48.660 --> 00:36:53.163
because up until fairly
recently, I mean, until today,

00:36:55.200 --> 00:37:00.200
logic is almost universally
regarded as being finite, okay?

00:37:00.300 --> 00:37:03.660
And yet I believe, as I have sketched,

00:37:03.660 --> 00:37:06.300
that there are good reasons
to think that it's infinite,

00:37:06.300 --> 00:37:10.680
both top-down about the nature
of logic and bottom-up about,

00:37:10.680 --> 00:37:14.100
which rest on much less
theoretical considerations

00:37:14.100 --> 00:37:15.093
about logic.

00:37:16.320 --> 00:37:18.750
Okay, so that was the
logic side of things,

00:37:18.750 --> 00:37:22.680
and then the last third
of the talk will be about

00:37:22.680 --> 00:37:25.530
non-deductive inference in mathematics,

00:37:25.530 --> 00:37:29.133
and here I will talk
squarely about inference,

00:37:30.210 --> 00:37:33.090
and this is the other side of the coin.

00:37:33.090 --> 00:37:34.740
The implication/inference distinction

00:37:34.740 --> 00:37:38.460
that I mentioned earlier will
not be very important here,

00:37:38.460 --> 00:37:40.830
and usually when applied to mathematics,

00:37:40.830 --> 00:37:42.180
this may be called informal

00:37:42.180 --> 00:37:45.600
or non-deductive inference or reasoning.

00:37:45.600 --> 00:37:49.053
So I'll switch between
those ways of speaking.

00:37:50.370 --> 00:37:52.203
Okay, so just to motivate this,

00:37:53.070 --> 00:37:54.690
let's think a little bit
about this simple example.

00:37:54.690 --> 00:37:56.940
Suppose, let's say, you're
a high school student

00:37:56.940 --> 00:37:58.140
and you're sort of playing around

00:37:58.140 --> 00:37:59.820
with the odd numbers, okay?

00:37:59.820 --> 00:38:01.710
And you notice a pattern, okay?

00:38:01.710 --> 00:38:04.440
You notice that 1, well,
of course that's 1 squared.

00:38:04.440 --> 00:38:08.670
Then you add 1 plus 3, 1 and
3 and you get 4, so 2 squared.

00:38:08.670 --> 00:38:11.370
1 plus 3 plus 5, you notice a 3 squared.

00:38:11.370 --> 00:38:13.620
1 plus 3 plus 5 plus 7 is 4 squared,

00:38:13.620 --> 00:38:14.850
and this is news to you, you know?

00:38:14.850 --> 00:38:17.190
You're not a mathematical whiz.

00:38:17.190 --> 00:38:18.810
You're a high school student

00:38:18.810 --> 00:38:20.610
who's never encountered this before,

00:38:20.610 --> 00:38:22.320
and so you form the conjecture

00:38:22.320 --> 00:38:27.320
that the sum of the first
n odd numbers is n squared,

00:38:28.290 --> 00:38:30.720
and then you might test
quite a few more cases.

00:38:30.720 --> 00:38:31.770
You might test them by hand.

00:38:31.770 --> 00:38:33.660
Maybe you'll, you know some coding,

00:38:33.660 --> 00:38:36.940
so you'll program a computer
to test the first billion cases

00:38:38.520 --> 00:38:41.280
and they all turn out to
verify this conjecture,

00:38:41.280 --> 00:38:42.113
but do you know it?

00:38:42.113 --> 00:38:43.350
Do you know the conjecture

00:38:43.350 --> 00:38:45.567
even if you check the first billion cases?

00:38:45.567 --> 00:38:48.183
And I think the answer's
a clear no, right?

00:38:49.102 --> 00:38:53.280
What you have is some good
but very partial evidence,

00:38:53.280 --> 00:38:55.770
and in particular, you
lack a proof, right?

00:38:55.770 --> 00:39:00.720
Until you prove the claim, it's
a very easy claim to prove,

00:39:00.720 --> 00:39:02.760
but we're assuming the high school student

00:39:02.760 --> 00:39:05.370
has not proved it at that point.

00:39:05.370 --> 00:39:07.860
When they haven't proved it,
just noticed the pattern,

00:39:07.860 --> 00:39:09.960
even for the first billion cases,

00:39:09.960 --> 00:39:12.540
they do not know the claim.

00:39:12.540 --> 00:39:13.443
They lack a proof.

00:39:15.180 --> 00:39:16.650
Okay, now, there are mathematically

00:39:16.650 --> 00:39:20.337
much more interesting examples
where we have more evidence,

00:39:20.337 --> 00:39:22.350
and not just a little bit more evidence,

00:39:22.350 --> 00:39:23.457
but a lot more evidence,

00:39:23.457 --> 00:39:26.460
and the example I wanna give you

00:39:26.460 --> 00:39:30.540
is that of Goldbach's
conjecture, call it GC,

00:39:30.540 --> 00:39:32.880
and it dates from 1742 in a letter

00:39:32.880 --> 00:39:35.880
by Christian Goldbach to Leonhard Euler.

00:39:35.880 --> 00:39:38.910
It states that every even
number greater than 2

00:39:38.910 --> 00:39:40.740
is the sum of two primes.

00:39:40.740 --> 00:39:42.150
Okay, so here's an example.

00:39:42.150 --> 00:39:44.580
6 is 3 plus 3 and that's the only way

00:39:44.580 --> 00:39:46.620
you can write 6 as the sum of two primes.

00:39:46.620 --> 00:39:50.445
3 is of course prime,
or you can write 100,

00:39:50.445 --> 00:39:54.330
or to take 100, you can
write that as 97 plus 3.

00:39:54.330 --> 00:39:56.490
That's one way of doing it for 100.

00:39:56.490 --> 00:39:59.820
Another way would be 53 plus 47

00:39:59.820 --> 00:40:01.890
and then there are lots of other ways.

00:40:01.890 --> 00:40:04.290
The interesting thing about
Goldbach's conjecture,

00:40:04.290 --> 00:40:08.010
despite the fact that it was conjectured

00:40:08.010 --> 00:40:12.633
almost 300 years ago,
it's currently unproved,

00:40:13.740 --> 00:40:17.190
but another interesting fact
about Goldbach's conjecture

00:40:17.190 --> 00:40:20.940
is that we have a lot, a
lot of evidence for it.

00:40:20.940 --> 00:40:23.250
So let me just run through
some of the evidence,

00:40:23.250 --> 00:40:24.083
not all of it.

00:40:24.083 --> 00:40:25.983
This is by no means an exhaustive list.

00:40:26.940 --> 00:40:29.580
The conjecture has been
checked for every even number

00:40:29.580 --> 00:40:31.790
up to 4 times 10 to the 18, right?

00:40:31.790 --> 00:40:35.493
So a lot more than 10,
than a billion cases.

00:40:36.900 --> 00:40:39.030
We also know it's also been proved

00:40:39.030 --> 00:40:41.700
that every sufficiently large even number

00:40:41.700 --> 00:40:43.320
is the sum of two numbers though.

00:40:43.320 --> 00:40:47.580
The first is a prime and the
second number is either a prime

00:40:47.580 --> 00:40:49.380
or the product of two primes.

00:40:49.380 --> 00:40:51.510
So that's getting very
close to the conjecture,

00:40:51.510 --> 00:40:53.010
and that fact has been proved.

00:40:54.180 --> 00:40:57.240
It's also been proved, and
this was actually in the 2010s,

00:40:57.240 --> 00:40:59.690
that every odd number is
the sum of three primes.

00:41:00.630 --> 00:41:04.143
Again, very close to the
conjecture, slightly weaker claim.

00:41:05.040 --> 00:41:06.750
It's also been proved that every number

00:41:06.750 --> 00:41:09.780
is the sum of no more than six primes.

00:41:09.780 --> 00:41:11.280
Okay, the Goldbach's conjecture, remember,

00:41:11.280 --> 00:41:13.620
says that it's exactly two.

00:41:13.620 --> 00:41:15.750
I mean, you can do it with just two.

00:41:15.750 --> 00:41:17.430
So again, this is a weaker claim,

00:41:17.430 --> 00:41:18.830
but that's also been proved.

00:41:20.910 --> 00:41:24.390
We also know in, for the
cases that we know about,

00:41:24.390 --> 00:41:27.093
for the up to the 4
times 10 to the 18 cases,

00:41:27.930 --> 00:41:30.420
not actually all of those
but quite a lot of those,

00:41:30.420 --> 00:41:32.940
number of ways that an
even number can be written

00:41:32.940 --> 00:41:36.660
as a sum of two primes sort of
non-monotonically increases.

00:41:36.660 --> 00:41:39.270
It doesn't increase in a
sort of monotonic fashion

00:41:39.270 --> 00:41:41.010
just going straight up, but you know,

00:41:41.010 --> 00:41:44.550
a bit like a currency
sort of strengthening,

00:41:44.550 --> 00:41:45.930
sort of goes up and down a little bit

00:41:45.930 --> 00:41:50.190
but with a kind of
underlying growing trend.

00:41:50.190 --> 00:41:52.830
So for example, in the case of 6,

00:41:52.830 --> 00:41:54.900
we saw that 6 can be
written in just one way

00:41:54.900 --> 00:41:58.770
as the sum of two primes, 3
plus 3, but 100 can be written,

00:41:58.770 --> 00:42:00.784
we gave at least two ways of doing it,

00:42:00.784 --> 00:42:02.340
and it can be written quite a few,

00:42:02.340 --> 00:42:05.133
as the sum of two primes
in quite a few ways,

00:42:06.060 --> 00:42:07.470
and in fact, when you get to a number

00:42:07.470 --> 00:42:09.810
even as small as about 10,000,

00:42:09.810 --> 00:42:12.960
that number can be written
as the sum of two primes

00:42:12.960 --> 00:42:16.770
in hundreds of ways, which
makes it very implausible

00:42:16.770 --> 00:42:20.100
that somehow this is
going to sort of crash

00:42:20.100 --> 00:42:22.170
from being able to write a number

00:42:22.170 --> 00:42:24.903
as a sum of several hundred,

00:42:25.920 --> 00:42:28.740
in several hundred ways
as a sum of two primes,

00:42:28.740 --> 00:42:31.230
the next even number,
somehow it's gonna crash

00:42:31.230 --> 00:42:33.270
and go down to to zero.

00:42:33.270 --> 00:42:35.280
It's a plausibility argument.

00:42:35.280 --> 00:42:37.863
It's not a proof, but that is the thought.

00:42:38.700 --> 00:42:40.380
There's also heuristic argument

00:42:40.380 --> 00:42:41.640
using the Prime Number Theorem,

00:42:41.640 --> 00:42:44.340
and the Prime Number
Theorem tells you about

00:42:44.340 --> 00:42:48.390
the distribution of primes
up to a particular N,

00:42:48.390 --> 00:42:53.390
tells you roughly how many
numbers smaller than N are prime,

00:42:53.670 --> 00:42:56.460
and it also, there's
an argument based on it

00:42:56.460 --> 00:42:58.410
that strongly supports the idea

00:42:58.410 --> 00:42:59.910
that Goldbach's conjecture is true.

00:42:59.910 --> 00:43:00.910
It doesn't prove it.

00:43:02.424 --> 00:43:04.980
It's based on how many numbers
smaller than N are prime,

00:43:04.980 --> 00:43:07.800
and if you look at the
sums of these numbers,

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:09.630
of these prime numbers, well,

00:43:09.630 --> 00:43:11.550
according to this heuristic argument,

00:43:11.550 --> 00:43:15.570
every typical integer that's
smaller or equal to 2N

00:43:15.570 --> 00:43:17.640
should be writeable as
the sum of two primes

00:43:17.640 --> 00:43:21.750
in sort of about N over
2 log N squared ways.

00:43:23.070 --> 00:43:24.390
So in other words,

00:43:24.390 --> 00:43:27.990
that should grow as N
gets bigger and bigger,

00:43:27.990 --> 00:43:31.080
and that's not a proof, but
it's a heuristic argument

00:43:31.080 --> 00:43:33.330
from the sort of
distribution of the primes.

00:43:33.330 --> 00:43:36.150
It's also been proved
that the counterexamples,

00:43:36.150 --> 00:43:38.550
the density tends to 0.

00:43:38.550 --> 00:43:41.400
That's to say at any given point,

00:43:41.400 --> 00:43:44.580
if you look at how many
numbers up to that point

00:43:44.580 --> 00:43:47.520
might be counterexamples
to the Goldbach conjecture,

00:43:47.520 --> 00:43:51.720
well, that ratio is gonna
definitely provably tend to 0,

00:43:51.720 --> 00:43:55.173
and there's more evidence
for Goldbach's conjecture.

00:43:56.280 --> 00:43:58.980
So as I say, lots of very strong evidence

00:43:58.980 --> 00:44:01.320
for the truth of Goldbach's conjecture

00:44:01.320 --> 00:44:03.000
that does not add up to a proof,

00:44:03.000 --> 00:44:05.670
and quite varied sort of evidence too,

00:44:05.670 --> 00:44:09.600
proofs of closely related
results, numerical evidence,

00:44:09.600 --> 00:44:12.183
heuristic arguments and so on,

00:44:13.823 --> 00:44:16.590
and there are lots of other
interesting conjectures

00:44:16.590 --> 00:44:17.610
in mathematics today.

00:44:17.610 --> 00:44:22.440
One is the, probably the most
famous unproved conjecture in,

00:44:22.440 --> 00:44:25.710
unproved statement in mathematics,
the Riemann hypothesis.

00:44:25.710 --> 00:44:27.360
You know, you'd have
to be more of an expert

00:44:27.360 --> 00:44:29.910
on that area of mathematics than I am

00:44:29.910 --> 00:44:34.047
to cite the evidence carefully there,

00:44:34.047 --> 00:44:36.570
and there are other sorts of arguments

00:44:36.570 --> 00:44:39.180
that are non-deductive in mathematics.

00:44:39.180 --> 00:44:42.243
Another well-known one
is primality testing,

00:44:44.040 --> 00:44:46.710
for example, the Rabin-Miller test,

00:44:46.710 --> 00:44:49.530
and this, the way this test works is

00:44:49.530 --> 00:44:52.683
you put forward a number N,

00:44:52.683 --> 00:44:55.920
a natural and a whole number N

00:44:55.920 --> 00:45:00.180
and then you run a test on
it to see whether it's prime,

00:45:00.180 --> 00:45:03.660
and you can do this several times,

00:45:03.660 --> 00:45:06.960
and every time you do it,
the result is, the claim,

00:45:06.960 --> 00:45:09.420
the verdict of whether
it's prime or not prime

00:45:09.420 --> 00:45:13.830
is independent of the previous
one if the number is prime,

00:45:13.830 --> 00:45:18.030
and so if you run the test 1,000
times with different inputs

00:45:18.030 --> 00:45:23.030
and 1,000 times the test
says that N is prime,

00:45:23.580 --> 00:45:25.980
then it's provable, it's provably the case

00:45:25.980 --> 00:45:28.830
that N is prime with this probability,

00:45:28.830 --> 00:45:33.150
a probability at least
1 minus 1/4 to 1,000,

00:45:33.150 --> 00:45:35.580
and that's just running
the test 1,000 times.

00:45:35.580 --> 00:45:37.200
Of course you can run it a lot more times

00:45:37.200 --> 00:45:39.510
and increase the exponent there.

00:45:39.510 --> 00:45:41.310
Now, 1 minus 1/4 to the 1,000

00:45:41.310 --> 00:45:43.470
is extremely close to 1, right?

00:45:43.470 --> 00:45:46.740
It's a degree of
confidence in N's primality

00:45:46.740 --> 00:45:50.010
that outstrips almost everything,

00:45:50.010 --> 00:45:52.440
any evidence that we have
with respect to anything

00:45:52.440 --> 00:45:56.490
in ordinary life and also in science.

00:45:56.490 --> 00:46:00.090
So we can be extremely, extremely
confident that N is prime.

00:46:00.090 --> 00:46:01.890
It's not a proof that N is prime,

00:46:01.890 --> 00:46:03.750
but it's a proof that N is prime

00:46:03.750 --> 00:46:06.543
with extremely, extremely
high probability.

00:46:07.440 --> 00:46:08.273
Okay.

00:46:10.170 --> 00:46:12.450
So by all normal and even
scientific standards,

00:46:12.450 --> 00:46:14.880
the evidence for the Goldbach conjecture

00:46:14.880 --> 00:46:17.280
is extraordinarily strong,

00:46:17.280 --> 00:46:20.010
but most mathematicians
nonetheless would say

00:46:20.010 --> 00:46:22.773
that we don't know Goldbach's conjecture,

00:46:24.000 --> 00:46:26.070
even the ones who have all the evidence,

00:46:26.070 --> 00:46:27.240
that's to say number theorists

00:46:27.240 --> 00:46:29.523
who know this evidence
about the conjecture.

00:46:31.290 --> 00:46:35.250
So I wanna suggest that actually
they're wrong about this.

00:46:35.250 --> 00:46:36.210
That in some cases,

00:46:36.210 --> 00:46:38.850
and probably in the case
of Goldbach's conjecture,

00:46:38.850 --> 00:46:41.130
and I'm gonna stick my neck out,

00:46:41.130 --> 00:46:43.173
we do know the truth of the claim,

00:46:44.580 --> 00:46:49.230
and what are the sorts of
considerations we can run here?

00:46:49.230 --> 00:46:52.830
Well, in the interests of time,
I'm just gonna state them.

00:46:52.830 --> 00:46:56.370
I'm not gonna go into them in any detail.

00:46:56.370 --> 00:46:58.500
The first consideration is actually

00:46:58.500 --> 00:47:00.630
that deduction doesn't have any sort of

00:47:00.630 --> 00:47:03.500
special epistemological virtue.

00:47:03.500 --> 00:47:06.900
So people have thought that
deduction preserves certainty.

00:47:06.900 --> 00:47:08.460
If you're certain of the premise,

00:47:08.460 --> 00:47:10.140
you should also be
certain of the conclusion,

00:47:10.140 --> 00:47:13.860
so there's something special
in that sense about deduction,

00:47:13.860 --> 00:47:16.713
but I don't think there's
good reason to think that,

00:47:18.270 --> 00:47:20.620
and just to run through
the second, the others,

00:47:21.720 --> 00:47:25.170
if you look at the sort
of analyses of knowledge

00:47:25.170 --> 00:47:28.440
in philosophy and
epistemology, they allow,

00:47:28.440 --> 00:47:31.680
they either allow for non-deductive
knowledge of mathematics

00:47:31.680 --> 00:47:34.769
or they just do not apply
to the case of mathematics.

00:47:34.769 --> 00:47:38.190
So general philosophers who
are not particularly interested

00:47:38.190 --> 00:47:40.380
in the philosophy of
mathematics have come up

00:47:40.380 --> 00:47:42.510
with conditions on knowledge.

00:47:42.510 --> 00:47:44.990
They don't seem to rule
out non-deductive knowledge

00:47:44.990 --> 00:47:48.327
of mathematics is the argument,

00:47:48.327 --> 00:47:50.940
the second consideration,
and the third consideration,

00:47:50.940 --> 00:47:54.630
that the axioms are the
foundation, are also justified,

00:47:54.630 --> 00:47:57.630
are often justified in
terms of their consequences.

00:47:57.630 --> 00:48:01.110
So it would be a bit odd
to justify some axioms

00:48:01.110 --> 00:48:02.610
of the foundation of mathematics,

00:48:02.610 --> 00:48:05.070
which is usually taken to be set theory,

00:48:05.070 --> 00:48:10.070
in terms of their consequences
that are typically more like

00:48:10.080 --> 00:48:13.920
scientific sort of evidence,
non-deductive evidence,

00:48:13.920 --> 00:48:17.400
but then insist that in order to know

00:48:17.400 --> 00:48:18.780
anything in mathematics,

00:48:18.780 --> 00:48:20.910
they must be provable from those axioms

00:48:20.910 --> 00:48:24.543
which are justified in this
sort of non-deductive way,

00:48:25.770 --> 00:48:27.870
and a fourth sort of consideration

00:48:27.870 --> 00:48:29.580
is that there are strong links between

00:48:29.580 --> 00:48:30.930
the mathematical and the physical.

00:48:30.930 --> 00:48:32.730
You can know applied mathematical facts

00:48:32.730 --> 00:48:34.140
by observation and testing.

00:48:34.140 --> 00:48:35.310
You know, you might be able to know

00:48:35.310 --> 00:48:39.480
what the mean of a
distribution is by sampling.

00:48:39.480 --> 00:48:40.623
You might be able to,

00:48:41.910 --> 00:48:45.300
you'd be able to work out some facts

00:48:45.300 --> 00:48:48.210
about geometrical facts by, for example,

00:48:48.210 --> 00:48:52.080
doing experiments on physical triangles.

00:48:52.080 --> 00:48:56.050
You might know numerical
facts by looking at clusters

00:48:56.050 --> 00:48:58.830
of physical objects and
so on, and in those cases,

00:48:58.830 --> 00:49:00.750
I think we very clearly want to say

00:49:00.750 --> 00:49:04.170
that in the physical
case, we know those facts,

00:49:04.170 --> 00:49:06.930
but then because they
sort of are analogous

00:49:06.930 --> 00:49:09.660
to mathematical facts,
it'll be strange to say

00:49:09.660 --> 00:49:11.940
that we know the physical facts.

00:49:11.940 --> 00:49:14.940
They have corresponding
mathematical facts,

00:49:14.940 --> 00:49:17.370
but yet we don't know those
corresponding mathematical facts

00:49:17.370 --> 00:49:18.900
if we haven't proved them.

00:49:18.900 --> 00:49:21.360
Okay, so I realized I've run
through those fairly quickly,

00:49:21.360 --> 00:49:23.943
but that's because I want to wrap up,

00:49:26.338 --> 00:49:29.190
and just very briefly as my last slide,

00:49:29.190 --> 00:49:30.960
what is the link between the two things

00:49:30.960 --> 00:49:32.430
that I've talked about, okay?

00:49:32.430 --> 00:49:33.690
So remember the first,

00:49:33.690 --> 00:49:37.470
my first argument was
that logic is infinite,

00:49:37.470 --> 00:49:40.230
against the sort of tenet
of contemporary thought

00:49:40.230 --> 00:49:43.590
that logic is finite, and
the second one was that,

00:49:43.590 --> 00:49:45.450
strictly about inference this time,

00:49:45.450 --> 00:49:49.770
that mathematical inference
is not as bad as it might seem

00:49:49.770 --> 00:49:51.810
and in fact can lead to knowledge.

00:49:51.810 --> 00:49:53.850
So maybe, I'm not entirely sure myself,

00:49:53.850 --> 00:49:56.670
but maybe what links them
is a sort of permissiveness

00:49:56.670 --> 00:49:58.570
about inference and about implication,

00:49:59.970 --> 00:50:01.940
and my claims are that there's much more

00:50:01.940 --> 00:50:03.210
to logic than recognized.

00:50:03.210 --> 00:50:04.380
It's infinite, and in fact.

00:50:04.380 --> 00:50:06.030
even highly infinite rather than finite,

00:50:06.030 --> 00:50:08.700
and that non-logical inference can be

00:50:08.700 --> 00:50:11.490
more reliable than is often appreciated,

00:50:11.490 --> 00:50:15.060
and even in mathematics where
the deductive method reigns,

00:50:15.060 --> 00:50:18.660
it should be recognized as
giving rise to knowledge.

00:50:18.660 --> 00:50:20.360
So thank you very much. That's it.

00:50:22.590 --> 00:50:25.800
- Great. Thank you so much, Alex.

00:50:25.800 --> 00:50:28.413
That was absolutely brilliant.

00:50:29.880 --> 00:50:34.200
We can now open up the floor to questions.

00:50:34.200 --> 00:50:36.453
Are there any questions?

00:50:38.790 --> 00:50:42.753
Please go ahead and
type in your questions.

00:50:46.880 --> 00:50:49.410
I'll just give people a couple of minutes

00:50:49.410 --> 00:50:52.413
to get their bearings.

00:50:53.970 --> 00:50:57.780
So if I may, (laughing) I may start,

00:50:57.780 --> 00:51:01.170
so what is special about

00:51:01.170 --> 00:51:04.890
the structure of mathematics?

00:51:04.890 --> 00:51:06.390
Obviously this is a big question,

00:51:06.390 --> 00:51:09.003
but there is something about,

00:51:11.220 --> 00:51:13.950
isn't there something about
the structure of mathematics

00:51:13.950 --> 00:51:18.950
that there is an inherent
provable self-consistency

00:51:20.640 --> 00:51:23.610
that you can demonstrate, right,

00:51:23.610 --> 00:51:25.833
say compared to, say, physics?

00:51:28.560 --> 00:51:30.993
- Yeah. Okay, thank you.

00:51:33.780 --> 00:51:36.870
I think what makes mathematics special

00:51:36.870 --> 00:51:41.100
is that it's so abstract,

00:51:41.100 --> 00:51:45.960
that it's about structure in general.

00:51:45.960 --> 00:51:49.920
So for example, it might
be about the structure

00:51:49.920 --> 00:51:53.100
of the natural numbers or it
might be the real numbers,

00:51:53.100 --> 00:51:56.250
but those can be described
in very abstract terms

00:51:56.250 --> 00:51:59.103
and they may have physical
instantiations or not,

00:52:00.540 --> 00:52:02.910
and that means because
we can characterize them

00:52:02.910 --> 00:52:04.653
in this very abstract way,

00:52:06.690 --> 00:52:10.570
then we can use abstract
arguments to discuss them

00:52:12.780 --> 00:52:16.020
and therefore makes the sort
of deductive method possible

00:52:16.020 --> 00:52:20.790
because you're just looking
at characterizing axioms.

00:52:20.790 --> 00:52:21.623
- Right.

00:52:21.623 --> 00:52:22.456
- [Alex] And that's all it takes

00:52:22.456 --> 00:52:23.880
to understand the structure.

00:52:23.880 --> 00:52:24.713
- Right,

00:52:27.810 --> 00:52:32.010
and the structure and
the sort of relationships

00:52:32.010 --> 00:52:36.843
between objects, mathematical objects,

00:52:37.680 --> 00:52:40.773
are somehow, they are more,

00:52:42.990 --> 00:52:43.823
they are not,

00:52:45.270 --> 00:52:48.960
they cannot be perturbed
by experimentation, right?

00:52:48.960 --> 00:52:51.930
So the analog that you have in physics,

00:52:51.930 --> 00:52:54.600
that you have a system,
especially, you know,

00:52:54.600 --> 00:52:57.390
quantum mechanics is
obviously the dramatic case,

00:52:57.390 --> 00:53:00.720
where, you know, the act
of making an observation

00:53:00.720 --> 00:53:03.330
alters the system itself, right?

00:53:03.330 --> 00:53:08.330
So there is something
about the stability, right,

00:53:08.700 --> 00:53:13.080
of mathematical objects that permits you

00:53:13.080 --> 00:53:16.440
to make these logical arguments.

00:53:16.440 --> 00:53:18.150
There's something about the fixity, right?

00:53:18.150 --> 00:53:19.070
So...

00:53:23.010 --> 00:53:24.120
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:53:24.120 --> 00:53:25.810
So I don't, I think there isn't

00:53:26.670 --> 00:53:29.550
any sort of observer interference

00:53:29.550 --> 00:53:32.373
of any kind in the mathematical case,

00:53:33.840 --> 00:53:36.330
and I mean that's, so
in the physical case,

00:53:36.330 --> 00:53:40.359
as you mentioned, there
is that famous instance,

00:53:40.359 --> 00:53:42.959
and of course it's a major
problem in social science

00:53:44.010 --> 00:53:47.310
that our role as theorists,

00:53:47.310 --> 00:53:49.110
social scientists who are theorists

00:53:49.110 --> 00:53:53.500
are involved in the
actual swirl of human life

00:53:54.660 --> 00:53:55.740
and therefore can affect it.

00:53:55.740 --> 00:53:58.080
So there isn't that sort
of feedback in any way

00:53:58.080 --> 00:53:59.730
and that maybe is one reason why

00:53:59.730 --> 00:54:01.710
that makes it sort of
cleaner and easier to study.

00:54:01.710 --> 00:54:04.120
- Cleaner, yeah, yeah.

00:54:04.120 --> 00:54:07.770
Yeah, I think we have a
question from Peter Morgan.

00:54:07.770 --> 00:54:10.680
What is the role of the axiom of choice

00:54:10.680 --> 00:54:13.110
in this kind of infinite logic?

00:54:13.110 --> 00:54:14.640
For the superhuman example,

00:54:14.640 --> 00:54:18.590
what difference does it make
if the superhuman misses out

00:54:18.590 --> 00:54:22.110
a non-procedurally determined
set of multiples of 2,

00:54:22.110 --> 00:54:24.093
if there's a gap, right?

00:54:25.710 --> 00:54:27.450
- Thank you very much, Peter Morgan.

00:54:27.450 --> 00:54:28.900
That's an excellent question.

00:54:31.473 --> 00:54:34.020
So the example that I
gave of the superhumans

00:54:34.020 --> 00:54:37.620
can be stated and fleshed out

00:54:37.620 --> 00:54:40.890
without using the axiom of choice,

00:54:40.890 --> 00:54:42.090
because you can say it,

00:54:42.090 --> 00:54:44.580
you can specify the rule that I gave.

00:54:44.580 --> 00:54:47.310
The inference that I mentioned

00:54:47.310 --> 00:54:50.220
can be described quite simply.

00:54:50.220 --> 00:54:53.970
You're just missing out the multiples of 2

00:54:53.970 --> 00:54:55.370
that are not multiples of 4.

00:54:56.520 --> 00:54:58.200
So you don't need the axiom of choice

00:54:58.200 --> 00:55:00.390
for that particular example.

00:55:00.390 --> 00:55:02.880
To answer the second
part of your question,

00:55:02.880 --> 00:55:05.580
what difference would it make
if a superhuman misses out

00:55:05.580 --> 00:55:08.790
a non-procedurally determined
set of multiples of 2,

00:55:08.790 --> 00:55:11.730
yes, in that case, in order to imagine

00:55:11.730 --> 00:55:13.170
an inference of that sort,

00:55:13.170 --> 00:55:15.843
you might have to invoke
the axiom of choice,

00:55:17.040 --> 00:55:19.503
depending on what is actually omitted,

00:55:20.700 --> 00:55:24.200
but the idea doesn't rely on it.

00:55:24.200 --> 00:55:25.770
It does not rely on the axiom of choice

00:55:25.770 --> 00:55:28.620
because you can just
describe it more simply

00:55:28.620 --> 00:55:29.703
in the way that I do.

00:55:31.230 --> 00:55:33.209
- The next question is from Kasavan,

00:55:33.209 --> 00:55:37.140
and is the claim here that
the high degree of certainty

00:55:37.140 --> 00:55:40.020
is or perhaps should be sufficient

00:55:40.020 --> 00:55:41.823
for mathematical knowledge?

00:55:43.609 --> 00:55:45.780
- Okay, Kasavan. Yes, thank you.

00:55:45.780 --> 00:55:47.393
No. My answer to that is no.

00:55:48.750 --> 00:55:51.513
I don't think high degree
of certainty is enough.

00:55:52.380 --> 00:55:56.163
I don't think that's
necessarily true in any sphere.

00:55:57.090 --> 00:55:59.523
It's you need varied evidence.

00:56:00.540 --> 00:56:01.980
I don't know how to,

00:56:01.980 --> 00:56:04.650
I don't think it's easy to
describe exactly what's required,

00:56:04.650 --> 00:56:06.750
but you need more than
high degree of certainty.

00:56:06.750 --> 00:56:08.760
You need varied evidence,

00:56:08.760 --> 00:56:11.280
so with evidence of different types,

00:56:11.280 --> 00:56:14.010
and evidence that would
convince, that would, you know,

00:56:14.010 --> 00:56:16.470
that many experts would
think would give you

00:56:16.470 --> 00:56:19.230
an extremely high justification.

00:56:19.230 --> 00:56:20.520
So just to give an example of

00:56:20.520 --> 00:56:22.683
why high degree of
certainty is not enough,

00:56:23.760 --> 00:56:24.960
in general I mean that, you know,

00:56:24.960 --> 00:56:27.270
the famous lottery cases described by,

00:56:27.270 --> 00:56:29.280
discussed by philosophers.

00:56:29.280 --> 00:56:30.603
You have a lottery ticket.

00:56:31.530 --> 00:56:34.230
You think you believe it's not gonna win.

00:56:34.230 --> 00:56:36.930
There are maybe a million lottery tickets,

00:56:36.930 --> 00:56:38.400
and, you know, you're right.

00:56:38.400 --> 00:56:39.840
It's not. It doesn't win.

00:56:39.840 --> 00:56:41.130
Did you know it?

00:56:41.130 --> 00:56:43.320
Well, you didn't really know it,

00:56:43.320 --> 00:56:46.230
even if your degree of
certainty was extremely high.

00:56:46.230 --> 00:56:50.880
So I want to say that high
degree of certainty is very,

00:56:50.880 --> 00:56:53.550
can help, but it's not enough.

00:56:53.550 --> 00:56:54.750
You need to look at the quality.

00:56:54.750 --> 00:56:56.760
It's not just the sort
of quantity, if you like,

00:56:56.760 --> 00:56:58.080
the quality of the evidence,

00:56:58.080 --> 00:57:00.357
but I do think that in
some cases, for example,

00:57:00.357 --> 00:57:02.760
the case of the Goldbach conjecture,

00:57:02.760 --> 00:57:06.030
probably we do have
that quality of evidence

00:57:06.030 --> 00:57:09.603
as well as the quantity that's
sufficient for knowledge.

00:57:11.460 --> 00:57:12.780
- Thanks.

00:57:12.780 --> 00:57:15.210
So the next question is
from Alexander Meehan.

00:57:15.210 --> 00:57:16.590
Thanks for a very clear talk.

00:57:16.590 --> 00:57:18.270
I'm curious to hear more about

00:57:18.270 --> 00:57:21.600
the connection between logic and inference

00:57:21.600 --> 00:57:24.870
and what the bridge principles
are if there are any.

00:57:24.870 --> 00:57:26.970
For example, I was
wondering if you could take

00:57:26.970 --> 00:57:29.750
your arguments that logic is infinitiary

00:57:30.810 --> 00:57:33.840
to show that deductive
inference is infinitiary,

00:57:33.840 --> 00:57:36.510
and related, what would it mean to say

00:57:36.510 --> 00:57:39.373
that deductive inference is infinitiary?

00:57:41.400 --> 00:57:42.770
- Thank you, Alexander.

00:57:44.400 --> 00:57:47.250
Good. I mean, I think these
are very difficult questions.

00:57:49.620 --> 00:57:51.060
There are bridge principles

00:57:51.060 --> 00:57:55.770
and it's actually an active
area of research in philosophy,

00:57:55.770 --> 00:57:57.450
or epistemology more specifically,

00:57:57.450 --> 00:57:59.040
to work out what are the bridge principles

00:57:59.040 --> 00:58:00.930
between implication and inference

00:58:00.930 --> 00:58:04.533
between logic and rational inference,

00:58:05.520 --> 00:58:09.900
but it's actually quite tricky
to spell them out precisely.

00:58:09.900 --> 00:58:11.700
So you might think it's something like,

00:58:11.700 --> 00:58:15.513
well, if you believe the premises,

00:58:18.818 --> 00:58:21.235
then you ought to believe the conclusion.

00:58:21.235 --> 00:58:23.310
So it will be something along those lines,

00:58:23.310 --> 00:58:26.165
but there's sort of
devil in the detail there

00:58:26.165 --> 00:58:29.040
and it's hard to work
out precisely what it is,

00:58:29.040 --> 00:58:30.890
but it's something along those lines.

00:58:32.160 --> 00:58:36.660
As for the second part, deductive
inference is infinitary,

00:58:36.660 --> 00:58:41.640
I've sort of did not want to
go that far, because as I said,

00:58:41.640 --> 00:58:43.440
deductive inference is something agents do

00:58:43.440 --> 00:58:46.080
and I find it hard, and yeah, you know,

00:58:46.080 --> 00:58:50.040
superhumans can carry
out infinitary reasoning,

00:58:50.040 --> 00:58:53.910
but we mere normal humans cannot,

00:58:53.910 --> 00:58:55.133
and so if deductive inference

00:58:55.133 --> 00:58:57.210
is very much tied to what we can do,

00:58:57.210 --> 00:59:01.380
even somewhat idealized
versions of us, then I'm not,

00:59:01.380 --> 00:59:03.690
I don't know if deductive
inference is infinitary.

00:59:03.690 --> 00:59:04.990
I don't wanna go that far.

00:59:08.160 --> 00:59:09.180
That's my answer.

00:59:09.180 --> 00:59:10.800
- Yeah. Thank you.

00:59:10.800 --> 00:59:15.800
So the question is about the
argument from topic neutrality.

00:59:16.320 --> 00:59:19.560
I see that to be able to
characterize topic neutrality,

00:59:19.560 --> 00:59:22.890
we need to make use of higher infinities,

00:59:22.890 --> 00:59:24.750
but does this mean that our logic

00:59:24.750 --> 00:59:27.600
needs to incorporate these infinities?

00:59:27.600 --> 00:59:29.760
And another version of
the same question maybe,

00:59:29.760 --> 00:59:32.070
is topic neutrality a neutral topic

00:59:32.070 --> 00:59:35.805
or is this special to the topic of logic?

00:59:35.805 --> 00:59:37.830
Is it tied, I guess, to logic, right?

00:59:37.830 --> 00:59:39.593
- I see. Okay, thank you, Mark.

00:59:41.747 --> 00:59:44.760
Right, so yeah.

00:59:44.760 --> 00:59:48.660
So it's not so much that to
characterize topic neutrality,

00:59:48.660 --> 00:59:51.510
we need to make use of higher infinities.

00:59:51.510 --> 00:59:55.410
It's more that when we spell out

00:59:55.410 --> 00:59:58.380
what topic neutrality means,

00:59:58.380 --> 01:00:01.140
what kind of expressions
or what kind of relations

01:00:01.140 --> 01:00:03.513
and properties are topic neutral,

01:00:06.067 --> 01:00:08.310
they are the relations
that can be expressed

01:00:08.310 --> 01:00:10.380
by an infinitary logic.

01:00:10.380 --> 01:00:11.880
So it comes by way of discovery.

01:00:11.880 --> 01:00:13.380
We're not using the higher infinities.

01:00:13.380 --> 01:00:15.150
They sort of come out of the argument.

01:00:15.150 --> 01:00:17.820
They tumble out of the argument,

01:00:17.820 --> 01:00:19.440
or another way of putting
it, if you just think about

01:00:19.440 --> 01:00:22.650
these infinite conjunctions
or disjunctions,

01:00:22.650 --> 01:00:26.430
they preserve, eliminating conjunction,

01:00:26.430 --> 01:00:29.700
infinitely many conjunctions
preserves topic neutrality.

01:00:29.700 --> 01:00:32.520
It's not specific if you
want a concrete example.

01:00:32.520 --> 01:00:35.763
It's not specific to
any particular domain.

01:00:36.840 --> 01:00:41.280
So that's how the argument
goes from topic neutrality,

01:00:41.280 --> 01:00:45.540
infinite, and highly
infinite logic falls out.

01:00:45.540 --> 01:00:47.820
Another version of the same
question maybe you asked,

01:00:47.820 --> 01:00:50.520
is topic neutrality a neutral topic

01:00:50.520 --> 01:00:53.010
or is it special to the topic of logic?

01:00:53.010 --> 01:00:54.120
Okay, that's a tricky one.

01:00:54.120 --> 01:00:56.703
We've got some self-reflection
going on there.

01:00:59.250 --> 01:01:03.502
Topic neutrality is a broad,

01:01:03.502 --> 01:01:05.460
I don't know how to answer that directly,

01:01:05.460 --> 01:01:07.230
but I'm not sure we need to.

01:01:07.230 --> 01:01:11.100
Topic neutrality is a feature,
a characteristic of logic.

01:01:11.100 --> 01:01:15.450
It is special to the topic
of logic. It doesn't apply.

01:01:15.450 --> 01:01:17.040
I suppose the answer is
maybe straightforward.

01:01:17.040 --> 01:01:18.450
I guess maybe I'm now thinking,

01:01:18.450 --> 01:01:19.830
I now think it may be straightforward.

01:01:19.830 --> 01:01:22.170
It is special to the topic of logic.

01:01:22.170 --> 01:01:25.410
Other topics are not topic neutral.

01:01:25.410 --> 01:01:27.750
You know, the law has a subject matter,

01:01:27.750 --> 01:01:29.970
particle physics has a subject matter,

01:01:29.970 --> 01:01:32.670
microeconomics has a
subject matter and so on,

01:01:32.670 --> 01:01:37.670
but logic is, seems like it's
the only branch that has,

01:01:37.710 --> 01:01:39.663
that is topic neutral.

01:01:41.970 --> 01:01:45.240
- Yeah, so then there's
a question from Joel.

01:01:45.240 --> 01:01:47.850
You distinguished
implication from inference

01:01:47.850 --> 01:01:50.610
as apples and oranges, I think,

01:01:50.610 --> 01:01:53.250
but wasn't your first
thesis about the first

01:01:53.250 --> 01:01:54.900
and your second about the second?

01:01:56.940 --> 01:01:58.264
- Yes.

01:01:58.264 --> 01:01:59.358
(Priya laughing)

01:01:59.358 --> 01:02:00.420
(laughing) Correct.

01:02:00.420 --> 01:02:02.280
My first thesis was about implication

01:02:02.280 --> 01:02:05.820
and my second one was about inference.

01:02:05.820 --> 01:02:06.813
They are closely,

01:02:07.710 --> 01:02:10.650
I want to be clear that they are distinct,

01:02:10.650 --> 01:02:13.710
but I also want to say that
they are closely related,

01:02:13.710 --> 01:02:16.440
and as I fessed up that my,

01:02:16.440 --> 01:02:20.850
I wanted to align this with
the topic of this series,

01:02:20.850 --> 01:02:25.020
so I snuck it into the title as inference.

01:02:25.020 --> 01:02:27.843
They are related, but
they are, but nonetheless,

01:02:29.010 --> 01:02:31.713
to be clear, they are distinct things.

01:02:36.480 --> 01:02:39.750
- Are there any other questions?

01:02:39.750 --> 01:02:43.353
Actually, Gila, if you have
a question, feel free to ask.

01:02:44.520 --> 01:02:47.070
- And maybe I'll keep the
questions for next time,

01:02:47.070 --> 01:02:51.980
but maybe just one small
thing about topic neutrality.

01:02:53.700 --> 01:02:56.430
It is very common in philosophy to say

01:02:56.430 --> 01:03:00.750
that a logic is topic neutral

01:03:00.750 --> 01:03:04.653
because it doesn't have
a topic of its own,

01:03:05.700 --> 01:03:08.283
but I think that this is not accurate.

01:03:09.690 --> 01:03:12.690
Logic is topic neutral in

01:03:12.690 --> 01:03:15.903
a logic does have a topic of its own

01:03:15.903 --> 01:03:20.340
and that is logical implication
or logical inference.

01:03:20.340 --> 01:03:25.340
No other discipline studies
this and logic studies this.

01:03:25.860 --> 01:03:30.860
So that's definitely a
distinct topic that logic has,

01:03:31.020 --> 01:03:35.190
but at the same time,
what is right about that

01:03:35.190 --> 01:03:39.360
and why it's important
that logic is topic neutral

01:03:39.360 --> 01:03:43.290
is that this topic, logical inference,

01:03:43.290 --> 01:03:46.830
applies to all fields of knowledge

01:03:46.830 --> 01:03:50.550
and so it applies to all topics.

01:03:50.550 --> 01:03:53.250
It applies to all other topics

01:03:53.250 --> 01:03:57.780
and that is because of
certain very special features

01:03:57.780 --> 01:04:00.600
of logic that apply to all topics.

01:04:00.600 --> 01:04:04.980
So, for example, physics
does not apply to numbers,

01:04:04.980 --> 01:04:09.570
but logic applies both to
numbers and to physical objects.

01:04:09.570 --> 01:04:13.050
So that's just a small comment.

01:04:13.050 --> 01:04:16.170
- Yeah, thanks for that
clarifying comment.

01:04:16.170 --> 01:04:18.990
So there is one more
question from Peter Morgan.

01:04:18.990 --> 01:04:22.500
So a differential equation
can be a model for,

01:04:22.500 --> 01:04:25.980
say, an electrical circuit or a pendulum.

01:04:25.980 --> 01:04:27.753
Is that not topic neutral?

01:04:29.700 --> 01:04:31.200
- Okay. Thank you, Peter.

01:04:31.200 --> 01:04:36.200
So I would say that it's
not exactly topic neutral.

01:04:36.390 --> 01:04:40.770
It's very general, but it's
not quite topic neutral.

01:04:40.770 --> 01:04:42.630
To take the straightforward case,

01:04:42.630 --> 01:04:44.373
maybe it's over the real numbers,

01:04:45.240 --> 01:04:49.320
and so to differentiate a
function over the real numbers,

01:04:49.320 --> 01:04:51.780
it's gotta be, you know,
a differentiable function,

01:04:51.780 --> 01:04:56.310
so it has to have a pretty
specific kind of form.

01:04:56.310 --> 01:04:59.820
It doesn't, the equation just can't apply

01:04:59.820 --> 01:05:03.180
to any phenomenon whatsoever.

01:05:03.180 --> 01:05:04.770
So it's very general, yes,

01:05:04.770 --> 01:05:07.563
but not quite as general as logic.

01:05:09.930 --> 01:05:11.340
- Right.

01:05:11.340 --> 01:05:15.960
So that was an interesting question

01:05:15.960 --> 01:05:17.657
to unpack, this, yeah.

01:05:17.657 --> 01:05:18.967
That was-
- Absolutely, yeah.

01:05:18.967 --> 01:05:22.080
Thank you, yeah.
- Thanks, Alex, yeah.

01:05:22.080 --> 01:05:24.690
So we have another question
from Alexander Meehan.

01:05:24.690 --> 01:05:26.610
So following up on a previous question,

01:05:26.610 --> 01:05:28.620
it wasn't obvious to me how evidence

01:05:28.620 --> 01:05:29.970
for the Goldbach conjecture

01:05:32.040 --> 01:05:36.213
defers nature from evidence
for winning a lottery.

01:05:37.350 --> 01:05:39.690
I have a different intuitive reaction,

01:05:39.690 --> 01:05:42.540
but can you give me a
more principled argument?

01:05:42.540 --> 01:05:45.933
It seemed to rely on diversity
of kinds of evidence.

01:05:48.900 --> 01:05:51.633
- Yes, okay. So it's good.

01:05:53.010 --> 01:05:56.343
So the, look, if the evidence for the,

01:05:57.480 --> 01:06:02.480
okay, so the evidence for the
diversity of kinds of evidence

01:06:02.760 --> 01:06:07.090
is crucial, I think, and
that is a big difference

01:06:08.250 --> 01:06:13.250
from the lottery case,
to the lottery case,

01:06:13.560 --> 01:06:16.500
and I think that's a
principle that we apply

01:06:16.500 --> 01:06:20.460
in all sorts of, in all
spheres of reasoning, right?

01:06:20.460 --> 01:06:21.810
I mean, for example, you know,

01:06:21.810 --> 01:06:24.570
testing the boiling point of water.

01:06:24.570 --> 01:06:27.030
I mean, if you, however
many times you test it,

01:06:27.030 --> 01:06:29.460
if you don't vary certain
conditions, right,

01:06:29.460 --> 01:06:31.620
if you don't vary the
pressure or the height

01:06:31.620 --> 01:06:33.633
or we always do it in the same room,

01:06:34.740 --> 01:06:37.620
then I don't think your
evidence is as good

01:06:37.620 --> 01:06:40.530
as if you did it perhaps fewer times

01:06:40.530 --> 01:06:44.520
but across a very
different range of contexts

01:06:44.520 --> 01:06:47.340
and changing other variables.

01:06:47.340 --> 01:06:48.840
So I think it is very relevant

01:06:49.717 --> 01:06:52.650
that you have all sorts of
different types of evidence.

01:06:52.650 --> 01:06:55.050
Now, the thing that I also
went over rather quickly,

01:06:55.050 --> 01:06:57.010
admittedly, towards the end of the talk

01:06:57.930 --> 01:07:00.150
was sort of more
philosophical considerations

01:07:00.150 --> 01:07:02.970
for how you might convert this idea

01:07:02.970 --> 01:07:07.890
of very strong and
diverse types of evidence

01:07:07.890 --> 01:07:11.070
into just an outright claim of knowledge,

01:07:11.070 --> 01:07:14.430
and I think really the onus is on someone

01:07:14.430 --> 01:07:17.370
who doesn't think this
to try and defend it,

01:07:17.370 --> 01:07:20.250
and the sorts of arguments
I gave there were, you know,

01:07:20.250 --> 01:07:23.760
the kind of arguments
that have come up in,

01:07:23.760 --> 01:07:25.410
the kind of ideas that
have come up in philosophy,

01:07:25.410 --> 01:07:28.290
for example, the nature of knowledge.

01:07:28.290 --> 01:07:30.630
Do you have, one sort of analysis

01:07:30.630 --> 01:07:34.200
of the nature of what
knowledge is is a popular one,

01:07:34.200 --> 01:07:36.960
which is not exactly right
but a sort of right track,

01:07:36.960 --> 01:07:40.410
is that it's a reliable true belief, okay?

01:07:40.410 --> 01:07:44.280
So if you have a true
belief about something

01:07:44.280 --> 01:07:46.890
and it's acquired by a reliable method,

01:07:46.890 --> 01:07:48.150
then that's knowledge.

01:07:48.150 --> 01:07:49.440
Now, that's not quite right.

01:07:49.440 --> 01:07:51.690
We know that from decades of epistemology,

01:07:51.690 --> 01:07:53.460
but it's along the right lines,

01:07:53.460 --> 01:07:58.083
and the method for
basing your mathematical,

01:07:59.700 --> 01:08:03.090
for inferring Goldbach's conjecture

01:08:03.090 --> 01:08:06.663
on this sort of evidence
is, I think, a reliable one,

01:08:07.590 --> 01:08:08.991
and again, it's, you know,

01:08:08.991 --> 01:08:09.930
I think it's important that there are

01:08:09.930 --> 01:08:11.550
these heuristic arguments, right,

01:08:11.550 --> 01:08:13.560
about the nature of the primes,

01:08:13.560 --> 01:08:15.780
how many primes there are up to N,

01:08:15.780 --> 01:08:17.760
and it would just be there's no reason,

01:08:17.760 --> 01:08:19.770
and you speak to a
mathematician, you know,

01:08:19.770 --> 01:08:20.847
and the mathematician would say,

01:08:20.847 --> 01:08:23.640
"There'd be no reason
to expect it not to hold

01:08:23.640 --> 01:08:25.920
given the distribution of the primes."

01:08:25.920 --> 01:08:30.390
So there are sort of inherent reasons here

01:08:30.390 --> 01:08:33.873
that go beyond just a lot of
cases, just sheer quantity.

01:08:40.950 --> 01:08:44.100
- There is, there are
some follow-up question,

01:08:44.100 --> 01:08:45.360
there's another follow-up question.

01:08:45.360 --> 01:08:46.760
I'll (indistinct) with that.

01:08:47.880 --> 01:08:49.680
I might have a diversity of evidence

01:08:49.680 --> 01:08:51.390
that I won't win the lottery.

01:08:51.390 --> 01:08:53.820
I've done many lotteries before and lost.

01:08:53.820 --> 01:08:55.620
There are lots of tickets in this lottery.

01:08:55.620 --> 01:08:58.800
I'm generally an unlucky
person, et cetera, et cetera,

01:08:58.800 --> 01:09:00.990
and it seems that the
process of concluding

01:09:00.990 --> 01:09:04.980
based on this kind of
evidence is a reliable one.

01:09:04.980 --> 01:09:05.850
- Yes, okay. Good.

01:09:05.850 --> 01:09:08.280
So the lottery cases show

01:09:08.280 --> 01:09:10.713
that it can't quite be reliability.

01:09:11.756 --> 01:09:15.420
There's gotta be more to
it than just reliability,

01:09:15.420 --> 01:09:17.703
but again, but the lottery cases,

01:09:19.410 --> 01:09:20.800
they're sort of insensitive

01:09:22.950 --> 01:09:25.260
to whether you hold the
winning ticket or not.

01:09:25.260 --> 01:09:26.093
I mean, you would still,

01:09:26.093 --> 01:09:29.130
even if you held the winning
ticket, you would still believe

01:09:29.130 --> 01:09:30.750
that you weren't gonna win the lottery,

01:09:30.750 --> 01:09:33.030
assuming you believe that
you didn't win the lottery.

01:09:33.030 --> 01:09:35.040
Just to get the structure of

01:09:35.040 --> 01:09:36.600
the alleged counterexample here,

01:09:36.600 --> 01:09:39.480
you're saying, "Well,
in the lottery cases,

01:09:39.480 --> 01:09:41.880
we don't want to say we
have reliable true belief

01:09:41.880 --> 01:09:42.870
that we're not gonna win,

01:09:42.870 --> 01:09:44.910
but the thought is we
don't have knowledge,"

01:09:44.910 --> 01:09:47.280
and that's correct, but
it's also insensitive

01:09:47.280 --> 01:09:50.880
to whether you have knowledge

01:09:50.880 --> 01:09:52.950
to whether you hold a
winning ticket or not,

01:09:52.950 --> 01:09:54.930
because if you did by chance
hold the winning ticket,

01:09:54.930 --> 01:09:56.490
you would still think the same things,

01:09:56.490 --> 01:09:58.590
but the mathematical case isn't like that.

01:10:00.855 --> 01:10:02.730
If Goldbach's conjecture were false,

01:10:02.730 --> 01:10:06.090
and that's a very hard thing to spell out,

01:10:06.090 --> 01:10:08.820
but we would expect quite
different sorts of evidence

01:10:08.820 --> 01:10:09.653
is the idea.

01:10:10.890 --> 01:10:11.723
- Right.

01:10:11.723 --> 01:10:14.970
So actually, I wanna go back to this point

01:10:14.970 --> 01:10:18.060
that you made about the, you know,

01:10:18.060 --> 01:10:21.330
our conception of the nature of knowledge

01:10:21.330 --> 01:10:23.910
and how reliable true beliefs,

01:10:23.910 --> 01:10:28.380
so if you look at physics,
right, so you are inherently,

01:10:28.380 --> 01:10:31.410
knowledge is inherently provisional.

01:10:31.410 --> 01:10:35.700
So are you making a claim that
mathematical knowledge is,

01:10:35.700 --> 01:10:38.583
doesn't have that kind of provisionality?

01:10:40.860 --> 01:10:42.270
That it's reliable, I mean,

01:10:42.270 --> 01:10:45.093
that reliability is uppercase R somehow?

01:10:46.470 --> 01:10:49.750
- No, in fact, I'm very drawn

01:10:50.646 --> 01:10:55.646
to a more scientific picture
of mathematical knowledge,

01:10:55.677 --> 01:10:57.540
and in a way that's why I'm,

01:10:57.540 --> 01:10:59.760
that's part of what's
in the background here.

01:10:59.760 --> 01:11:00.780
- Okay. Yeah.

01:11:00.780 --> 01:11:03.450
- That I think mathematics is a bit more,

01:11:03.450 --> 01:11:05.400
at least a bit more like science,

01:11:05.400 --> 01:11:08.700
natural science, than most people think,

01:11:08.700 --> 01:11:11.070
and of course this sort
of non-deductive reasoning

01:11:11.070 --> 01:11:12.990
is all over the place in science.

01:11:12.990 --> 01:11:13.823
- Right.

01:11:15.600 --> 01:11:19.500
- Yeah, and I think the
history of mathematics

01:11:19.500 --> 01:11:22.050
shows that mathematics, I mean,

01:11:22.050 --> 01:11:24.750
it's more cumulative than science,

01:11:24.750 --> 01:11:26.730
but it's not entirely cumulative.

01:11:26.730 --> 01:11:29.080
You know, there are
revolutions in mathematics.

01:11:30.180 --> 01:11:31.800
There are things that we believed,

01:11:31.800 --> 01:11:33.150
that everyone believed, you know,

01:11:33.150 --> 01:11:36.540
that all numbers were positive,

01:11:36.540 --> 01:11:39.120
or all numbers were real for centuries,

01:11:39.120 --> 01:11:41.250
or that, you know, the parallel postulate,

01:11:41.250 --> 01:11:44.670
or Euclid in one of
his common notions says

01:11:44.670 --> 01:11:47.760
that the whole is bigger than its parts.

01:11:47.760 --> 01:11:49.200
Actually, set theory and one way

01:11:49.200 --> 01:11:51.050
of understanding that disproves that.

01:11:52.050 --> 01:11:56.190
So I do not, I don't have
that picture of mathematics

01:11:56.190 --> 01:11:58.977
as sort of eternal in
that way, in (indistinct).

01:12:01.470 --> 01:12:05.250
- Yeah, and that's, okay,
now that kind of fits while,

01:12:05.250 --> 01:12:07.770
so that's why you're making a claim,

01:12:07.770 --> 01:12:11.190
sort of you're making the
sort of distinguishing between

01:12:11.190 --> 01:12:15.270
sort of deductive and the
non-deductive part of, okay,

01:12:15.270 --> 01:12:16.950
so you're kind of making room

01:12:16.950 --> 01:12:21.360
for a more provisional, yeah, okay.

01:12:21.360 --> 01:12:24.930
- Absolutely, so I want to, I'm drawn,

01:12:24.930 --> 01:12:26.910
I'm trying to assimilate
mathematics to science

01:12:26.910 --> 01:12:29.760
because that's where I think it is closer

01:12:29.760 --> 01:12:31.080
to science than we think.

01:12:31.080 --> 01:12:32.340
- Okay.

01:12:32.340 --> 01:12:33.921
- [Alex] In its method.

01:12:33.921 --> 01:12:35.462
- Okay.

01:12:35.462 --> 01:12:39.753
Okay, are there any other questions?

